{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9859211f294a65b3", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data\n2\tAugmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n3\tEoin Cummins∗\n4\t\n5\tZhongyi Huang∗\n6\t\n7\tAlexandre D’Hooge\n8\t\n9\tUniversity College Dublin\n10\tDublin, Ireland\n11\t\n12\tGuangxi Normal University\n13\tGuilin, China\n14\t\n15\tGreat Bay University\n16\tDongguan, Guangdong, China\n17\t\n18\tZhuoro Mo∗\n19\t\n20\tYaolong Ju†\n21\t\n22\tShenzhen University\n23\tShenzhen, China\n24\t\n25\tGreat Bay University\n26\tDongguan, Guangdong, China\n27\tjuyaolong@gbu.edu.cn\n28\t\n29\tarXiv:2608.06165v1 [cs.SD] 6 Aug 2026\n30\t\n31\tAbstract\n32\tExisting audio-to-score (A2S) systems primarily focus on classical\n33\tmusic, and the application to popular music remains underexplored.\n34\tThis paper first presents the new SheetSage-A2S Dataset, which\n35\tincludes 61 hours of audio with **kern score encodings for 9,468\n36\tclips originating from 6,066 unique songs, the first of its kind to\n37\tfacilitate A2S research for popular music. Additionally, we improve\n38\ton existing A2S approaches by using data augmentation and MuQ,\n39\ta pretrained feature-extraction model for music audio, to enhance\n40\tgeneralisation abilities and extract meaningful audio features. Results show that the proposed A2S model achieves 4.98% symbol\n41\terror rate (SER) on the Quartets collection for classical music, which\n42\tsignificantly outperforms the 15.3% SER from the existing stateof-the-art [1]. Additionally, our model achieves 20.92% SER on\n43\tthe SheetSage-A2S dataset for popular music, serving as a strong\n44\tbenchmark for future research. The dataset, model, and code are\n45\tmade publicly available at: https://github.com/Multimodal-MusicResearch-Lab/SheetSage2Kern_model.\n46\t\n47\tCCS Concepts\n48\t• Information systems → Music retrieval; • Applied computing → Sound and music computing; • Computing methodologies\n49\t→ Feature selection.\n50\t\n51\tKeywords\n52\tAudio-to-Score Transcription, Datasets, Lead Sheets, Pre-trained\n53\tAudio Features\n54\tACM Reference Format:\n55\tEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju. 2026. Audio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features,\n56\tData Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset. In Proceedings\n57\tof the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM ’26), November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. ACM, New York, NY, USA, 9 pages.\n58\thttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835653\n59\t\n60\t∗ Also with Great Bay University.\n61\t† Corresponding author: juyaolong@gbu.edu.cn\n62\t\n63\tCopyright held by the owner/author(s). This is the author’s version of the work. It is posted here for\n64\tyour personal use. Not for redistribution. The definitive Version of Record was published in\n65\tProceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM ’26),\n66\thttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835653.\n67\t\n68\tSymbolic\n69\tRepresentation\n70\t\n71\tAudio File\n72\t\n73\tNote-level\n74\tAMT\n75\tModel\n76\t\n77\tScore\n78\tDecoder\n79\tA#dim7\n80\t\n81\tA2S\n82\tModel\n83\t\n84\tF#7/A#\n85\t\n86\tSymbolic Score\n87\t(kern, MusicXML, MEI,...)\n88\t\n89\tFigure 1: Illustration of the two main ways to generate symbolic music scores: (top) modular approach based on a traditional, note-level AMT model that typically outputs symbolic\n90\trepresentations such as MIDI or piano-roll; (bottom) A2S approach that directly generates score symbols based on the\n91\tinput audio file.\n92\t\n93\t1\n94\t\n95\tIntroduction\n96\t\n97\tAudio-to-Score Transcription (A2S) can be considered as a subfield\n98\tof Automatic Music Transcription (AMT). Indeed, as defined in\n99\t[3], AMT consists in “the design of computational algorithms to\n100\tconvert acoustic music signals into some form of musical notation”.\n101\tHowever, more often than not, the output of AMT models is a\n102\tMIDI or piano-roll representation [3, 14], and not a full-fledged\n103\tmusical score. A2S research addresses this limitation, by setting\n104\tthe desired output as a human-readable score. In this section, we\n105\tfirst introduce related work in the field of A2S and limitations of\n106\texisting approaches, before presenting our proposed approach and\n107\tcontributions.\n108\t\n109\t1.1\n110\t\n111\tRelated Work\n112\t\n113\tObtaining a formatted score can be done in a modular or end-to-end\n114\tapproach, as illustrated in Figure 1. Modular approaches involve\n115\tconverting a MIDI or piano-roll intermediate representation to\n116\tsymbolic scores. This can be done with rule-based algorithms, as\n117\tin [9] where the authors compute meter, key signature, and note\n118\tspellings from MIDI files, and engrave the scores with Lilypond.\n119\tMIDI-to-score conversion can also be formulated as a data-driven,\n120\tsequence-to-sequence problem, as in [4], where a transformer\n121\tmodel produces MusicXML files from performance MIDI recordings.\n122\tA limitation of modular approaches is that they can accumulate\n123\terrors throughout their processing stages, unlike end-to-end approaches where audio is directly converted into a typeset score.\n124\t\n125\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n126\t\n127\tEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n128\t\n129\tTable 1: Datasets used in A2S research. RISM files are incipits of unknown durations. All datasets are Western classical music\n130\tscores except MuseSyn that includes piano arrangements of other musical styles, and our new dataset that gathers lead sheets\n131\tof popular music. Although our dataset contains audio clips with singing vocals and accompaniment, a polyphonic texture\n132\tsimilar to the Quartets, Chorales, and MuseSyn datasets, the corresponding scores of SheetSage-A2S contain the monophonic\n133\tsinging melodies as the essential part of lead sheets.\n134\tDataset\n135\t\n136\tNum. Files\n137\t\n138\tDuration\n139\t\n140\tRecordings\n141\t\n142\tScore Formats\n143\t\n144\tMusic Texture\n145\t\n146\tRISM [26]\n147\t\n148\t246,870\n149\t\n150\tUnknown\n151\t\n152\tSynthetic\n153\t\n154\tPAE [7] incipits\n155\t\n156\tMonophonic\n157\t\n158\tQuartets [25]\n159\t\n160\t34,512\n161\t\n162\t20.25h\n163\t\n164\tSynthetic\n165\t\n166\t**kern\n167\t\n168\tPolyphonic\n169\t\n170\tChorales [25]\n171\t\n172\t352\n173\t\n174\t5.79h\n175\t\n176\tSynthetic\n177\t\n178\t**kern\n179\t\n180\tPolyphonic\n181\t\n182\tMuseSyn [18]\n183\t\n184\t210\n185\t\n186\t9.62h\n187\t\n188\tSynthetic\n189\t\n190\tMusicXML\n191\t\n192\tPolyphonic\n193\t\n194\tSheetSage-A2S\n195\t(Ours)\n196\t\n197\t9,468\n198\t\n199\t61h\n200\t\n201\tReal\n202\t\n203\t**kern\n204\t\n205\tPolyphonic\n206\t\n207\tConvolutional and recurrent neural networks have been used in\n208\t[8, 24–26] for such end-to-end conversions, as well as attentionbased models in [1, 17].\n209\tThe current A2S research faces two major limitations. One is\n210\tthe lack of datasets, which has been highlighted in recent papers\n211\t[2, 26], as many datasets include either audio files or symbolic\n212\tscores but rarely both.1 One way of circumventing this problem is\n213\tto generate audio files from symbolic scores using audio synthesis\n214\twith virtual instrument sounds. The humdrum-data repository2 is\n215\ttherefore sometimes used as a source of music scores in the **kern\n216\tformat [28] to build synthetic A2S datasets such as Quartets and\n217\tChorales, used in [1, 2, 25]. Other public music scores websites\n218\tlike the RISM and Musescore catalogues are used in [18, 26], but\n219\tthe audio files are still obtained through digital synthesis. While\n220\tsynthesis facilitates dataset creation, resorting to such recordings is\n221\ta significant drawback that questions the ability of previous work to\n222\tgeneralise to real recordings. Furthermore, all the aforementioned\n223\tpapers study Western Classical Music, leaving the vast genre of\n224\tPopular Music mostly untouched.\n225\tTable 1 summarises the characteristics of datasets used in previous A2S research.\n226\tThe other limitation is the lack of exploiting popular machine\n227\tlearning techniques to improve performance, such as the use of\n228\tpre-trained models and data augmentation.\n229\tPre-trained models proved useful in AMT, as [23] finetune a\n230\tmodel trained on piano music and attain state-of-the-art performance on guitar music. The student-teacher learning paradigm can\n231\talso be used and showed promising results in drums transcription\n232\t[34]. Recently, new music foundation models have also been proposed, which offer learned representations that can be applied to\n233\tdownstream tasks [19]. Choosing the right pre-trained model for\n234\ta downstream task is not straightforward, which is why benchmarks like MARBLE [36] are useful to assess the performance of\n235\ta model. This benchmark uses 12 datasets to evaluate models on\n236\t18 musical understanding tasks like key detection, chord estimation or source separation. Examples of models performing well on\n237\t\n238\tInstruments\n239\tPiano, Harpsichord,\n240\tOrgan, Strings, Winds\n241\tString quartets\n242\tSoprano, Alto, Tenor,\n243\tBass voices\n244\tPiano\n245\tSinging vocals for\n246\tmelodies\n247\t\n248\tMARBLE are MERT [16], MusicFM [33], and MuQ [38]. All three\n249\tmodels are trained using masked language modelling, but with\n250\tdifferent tokenisation strategies. Using a music foundation model is\n251\ta promising way of improving A2S models, as transcribing recordings to human-readable scores involves several complex processes,\n252\tsuch as pitch and chord recognition, beat detection, and key finding.\n253\tRegarding data augmentation, common techniques consist of applying controlled pitch-shifting or time-stretching to audio recordings\n254\t[21]. These augmentations were shown to increase performance in\n255\tAMT-related tasks, including transcriptions of lyrics [37], drums\n256\t[13], piano [11], or classical music pieces with varied instrumentations [29]. Nevertheless, we could not find A2S papers that use\n257\teither pre-trained models or such data augmentation techniques.\n258\t\n259\t1.2\n260\t\n261\tContributions\n262\t\n263\tIn this paper, we address the data scarcity limitation by proposing\n264\tthe new SheetSage-A2S dataset, which includes 61 hours of audio\n265\twith 9,468 **kern score encodings for 6,066 unique songs,3 the\n266\tfirst of its kind to facilitate A2S research for popular music. The\n267\tscores in SheetSage-A2S are lead sheets, i.e. scores that engrave the\n268\tmelody and chords for popular music and jazz songs [10, 20]. A lead\n269\tsheet excerpt is shown on the right of Figure 1, where the melody\n270\tis specified in the staff while chords are attached above the melody.\n271\tUnlike existing A2S datasets, all music from our SheetSage-A2S\n272\tdataset contains real recordings released commercially that can\n273\tbe found on YouTube, or other music streaming services. Another\n274\tdifference is that our dataset isolates the melody tracks, which\n275\tare all sung by singers, and exclusively records their information\n276\tfrom polyphonic mixtures, in contrast to the existing databases\n277\tthat transcribe and notate every individual note of relevant musical\n278\tinstruments.\n279\tWe also address the performance ceiling by proposing our new\n280\tA2S model, which adopts an autoregressive transformer decoder\n281\tsimilar to [1], and make three modifications (shown in\n282\tFigure 5\n283\t). First, we increase the capacity and stability of the original decoder by expanding its feedforward dimension from 256 to 1024 and\n284\t\n285\t1 See for instance https://www.audiocontentanalysis.org/datasets (all web links were\n286\t\n287\tlast accessed and verified in March 2026).\n288\t2 https://github.com/humdrum-tools/humdrum-data\n289\t\n290\t3 Each encoding represents a section (e.g. chorus, verse) of the song. Therefore, multiple\n291\t\n292\tencodings may belong to the same song.\n293\t\n294\t\fAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n295\t\n296\tData Acquisition\n297\t\n298\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n299\t\n300\tOctave Inference\n301\t\n302\tKern Conversion\n303\t\n304\tPitch map creation\n305\tClip\n306\t\n307\tKern\n308\tannotations\n309\t\n310\tHypothesis\n311\tselection\n312\t\n313\tRMVPE\n314\tprobabilities\n315\t\n316\tVocal stem\n317\textraction\n318\t\n319\tOctave\n320\tfrom best\n321\thypothesis\n322\tchosen\n323\t\n324\tRMS >\n325\t0.0005?\n326\tDownload\n327\taudio\n328\t\n329\tC > 0.2?\n330\tJson2Kern\n331\tHypothesis generation\n332\t\n333\tExisting JSON from [10]\n334\twith refined beat timings\n335\t\n336\t12 hypotheses\n337\t\n338\tFigure 2: Overview of the SheetSage-A2S dataset creation pipeline, with three main steps that are introduced in detail at\n339\tsection 2. The confidence score 𝐶 measures how well a potential octave and starting beat align with RMVPE’s [31] vocal pitch\n340\testimates. The process of Json2Kern is further illustrated in Figure 3.\n341\tadopting a Pre-Norm architecture. Second, we replace the vanilla\n342\tCNN encoder with MuQ [38], a pre-trained model that employs\n343\tself-supervised learning to extract rich audio features for music understanding. Finally, we employ data augmentation, including pitch\n344\tshifting and time stretching, to improve robustness and increase\n345\tthe amount of data for training.\n346\tThe experimental results demonstrate that the proposed A2S\n347\tmodel achieves significant performance improvements over the\n348\tcurrent state-of-the-art method [1] on the Quartets dataset for\n349\tclassical music. Meanwhile, it obtains a Symbol Error Rate (SER)\n350\tof 20.92% on the new SheetSage-A2S dataset for popular music,\n351\testablishing a strong benchmark for future research in this domain.\n352\tThe organisation of the rest of this paper is described hereafter. Section 2 presents the SheetSage-A2S dataset. Section 3 describes the baseline model and our proposed architecture. Section 4\n353\tpresents the experiments, and Section 5 reports the results and ablation studies. Section 6 discusses the results, and Section 7 concludes\n354\tthe paper and proposes directions for future research.\n355\t\n356\t2\n357\t\n358\tDataset\n359\t\n360\tWe introduce SheetSage-A2S, a dataset for popular music with\n361\taudio-to-score transcription built from the original SheetSage annotations [10] and corresponding audio. At SheetSage, each annotation represents a distinct segment (such as a verse or chorus) from\n362\ta particular song. We therefore refer to these individual segments\n363\tas “clips”. The dataset contains 9,468 clips derived from 6,066 songs\n364\tby 2,891 artists. The mean clip duration is approximately 23 seconds, yielding 61 hours of audio in total. Each example pairs an\n365\taudio clip with a **kern score obtained from the source annotations, and we choose **kern as the score encoding format due to\n366\tits simple and compact form, compared to other formats such as\n367\tMusicXML.4 Our dataset release contains no copyrighted audio: we\n368\t4 https://www.musicxml.com/\n369\t\n370\tdistribute the **kern annotations under CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 (similar\n371\tto the original SheetSage), along with YouTube links, timestamps,\n372\tand durations. We also provide pre-computed audio features for\n373\tall clips. The whole dataset creation pipeline for SheetSage-A2S\n374\tis shown in Figure 2, where we first acquire the data from SheetSage (Section 2.1), then we clean the data by removing all the clips\n375\twithout melodies or chords. For the remaining clips, we obtain the\n376\tpitches for all singing melodies via the process of octave inference\n377\t(Section 2.2). Finally, we compile all relevant music elements for\n378\teach clip from the JSON file and convert them into the resulting\n379\t**kern annotations (Section 2.3), which are combined with the\n380\tdownloaded audio to form the proposed SheetSage-A2S dataset.\n381\t\n382\t2.1\n383\t\n384\tData Acquisition\n385\t\n386\tWe follow [10] and use their open-source JSON file,5 which contains\n387\tclips of annotations as well as the corresponding YouTube links for\n388\tmusic audio, as a starting point. We attempt to scrape all the audio\n389\tfiles with YouTube links using the yt-dlp6 toolkit. There are 26,175\n390\tentries in the original JSON file, and we managed to download\n391\t20,072 audio clips. Among the 6,103 failed cases, around 4000 clips\n392\tare not available anymore, the remaining ones are not made public\n393\tdue to copyright reasons.\n394\tWithin the JSON file provided by [10], the onsets and offsets\n395\tfor each note and chord are specified in terms of musical beats\n396\trather than exact timestamps in seconds. To align these symbolic\n397\tannotations with the downloaded audio, the JSON includes refined\n398\tbeat-to-time mappings generated using the beat and downbeat\n399\tdetection algorithms [6] from the madmom library [5], which has\n400\tbeen demonstrated to be highly reliable for mapping beat positions\n401\tto precise timestamps in seconds. We adopt the formalisation from\n402\t5 https://github.com/chrisdonahue/sheetsage-data/blob/main/hooktheory/\n403\t\n404\tHooktheory.json.gz\n405\t6 https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp\n406\t\n407\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n408\t\n409\tEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n410\t\n411\t[10] and define this function as an alignment function 𝐴 : [0, 𝐵) →\n412\t[0,𝑇 ) that assigns each of 𝐵 beats in the metrical structure to a time\n413\t𝑡 ∈ [0,𝑇 ) in the audio.\n414\t\n415\t2.2\n416\t\n417\tjson\n418\t\n419\tOctave Inference\n420\t\n421\t𝑡𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 (𝑠) = 𝐴(𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 ) − 𝑠,\n422\t\n423\t𝑡𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 (𝑠) = 𝐴(𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 ) − 𝑠\n424\t\n425\tEach combination of (𝑂, 𝑠) defines a hypothesis index mask: the\n426\tset of cells in 𝑃 that the notes would occupy under that hypothesis,\n427\tshown as the red rectangles in Figure 2:\n428\t𝐻 (𝑂, 𝑠) =\n429\t\n430\tØ\b\n431\t\n432\tMetadata\n433\t\n434\tMelody\n435\t\n436\tOne obstacle encountered during the step of data acquisition is that\n437\tthe melodies written on the original SheetSage annotation use a\n438\trelative octave system rather than an absolute one.7 As a result, the\n439\texact pitch of each note from the melody is unknown, preventing\n440\tus from obtaining symbolic scores.\n441\tPitch map creation. To overcome this issue and obtain absolute\n442\toctave information, we use existing pitch estimation tools to obtain\n443\tfundamental frequencies (F0) for singing melodies,8 which are first\n444\textracted using a\n445\tsource separation tool known as the Mel-band Roformer [30]\n446\tfor each audio clip. We then eliminate all silent clips and retain\n447\tthe ones whose singing RMS (Root Mean Square) energy exceeds\n448\t0.0005, resulting in 16,310 candidate clips. Next, we adopt RMVPE\n449\t[31] as our pitch estimation tool and apply it to the isolated singing\n450\tmelodies, resulting in\n451\t72 semitone-spaced bins\n452\tthat form a pitch probability matrix 𝑃 ∈ [0, 1]𝑇 ×72 , where T\n453\tdenotes the number of time frames and the y-axis contains semitonespaced frequency probabilities from the note range within C1-B6.\n454\tHypothesis generation. Let 𝑁 denote the set of annotated\n455\tnotes in the annotated melody, where each note 𝑛 ∈ 𝑁 has a relative\n456\tpitch 𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑙,𝑛 and beat-indexed onset and offset [𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 , 𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 ]. Each\n457\trelative pitch 𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑙,𝑛 is made up of the note’s pitch class (an integer\n458\tfrom 0 to 11) and the note’s relative octave (an integer typically\n459\tbetween -1 and 1, which allows users to enter for example an\n460\tE and then an E the next octave up or down). To determine the\n461\tmost probable octave, we first treat the note’s relative pitch as\n462\tan absolute pitch. We then shift it upwards by an octave for all\n463\toctaves within RMVPE’s output range. We test all combinations\n464\tof six octave shifts 𝑂 ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5} and two start time offsets\n465\t𝑠: the madmom detected downbeat start timestamp and the userdefined start timestamp. Since the alignment function 𝐴 maps beats\n466\tto global time, but our pitch matrix 𝑃 is for a trimmed clip, we\n467\tsubtract 𝑠 to find the notes’ local positions. We test both offsets\n468\tbecause madmom can sometimes miss the first beat (e.g. if a clip\n469\tbegins with a held note), which shifts the entire melody.\n470\tFor each note we then calculate the start and end time as:\n471\t\n472\t(𝑡, 𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑙,𝑛 + 12 · 𝑂) | 𝑡 ∈ [𝑡𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 (𝑠), 𝑡𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 (𝑠)]\n473\t\n474\t𝑛∈𝑁\n475\t7 This means that the annotation only contains the pitch class and its octave relative to\n476\t\n477\tother notes.\n478\t8We choose to focus singing melodies rather than melodies performed by instruments\n479\tin this work, because existing pitch estimation tools [15, 31, 32] only used synthesised\n480\tinstruments for testing, which may not generalise well on our dataset that contain\n481\treal recordings for commercial music.\n482\t\n483\tPitch classes, onsets\n484\tand offsets\n485\t\n486\tNote spelling\n487\t\n488\ttitle\n489\tartist\n490\thooktheory-id\n491\tyoutube-url\n492\tkey\n493\t\n494\t1\n495\t\n496\tChords\n497\tRoot notes, intervals,\n498\tinversions, onsets and\n499\toffsets\n500\t\n501\tRoot note\n502\tspelling\n503\tOther tones\n504\tspelling\n505\t\n506\tmeter\n507\t\n508\tChord labelling\n509\t\n510\tTemporal\n511\tAlignment\n512\tRhythm\n513\tQuantisation\n514\t\n515\t2\n516\t\n517\t3\n518\t\n519\t[header]\n520\t\n521\t**kern\n522\t[melody\n523\tspine]\n524\t\n525\t**cdata\n526\t[chord\n527\tspine]\n528\t\n529\t[footer]\n530\t\n531\t4 Symbolic Score\n532\t\n533\tFigure 3: Summary of the json to **kern conversion pipeline.\n534\tNumbers denote the four main processing steps described in\n535\tSection 2.3.\n536\tCombining six octave shifts with two start time offsets gives 12\n537\thypotheses per clip.\n538\tHypothesis selection. We score each hypothesis by the probability mass from 𝑃 that falls inside its mask, normalised by total\n539\tnote duration:\n540\tÍ\n541\t(𝑡, 𝑝 ) ∈ 𝐻 𝑃 (𝑡, 𝑝)\n542\t\u0001\n543\t𝐶 (𝐻 ) = Í\n544\t𝑡\n545\t𝑛∈𝑁 𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 (𝑠) − 𝑡𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 (𝑠)\n546\tThe starting time and octave from the hypothesis with the highest confidence are selected as the clip’s octave and starting time. We\n547\tdiscard clips with confidence 𝐶 = argmax 𝐶 (𝐻 ) below a threshold\n548\t𝐻\n549\t\n550\tof 0.2 for the following reasons. First, manual inspection revealed\n551\tthat confidence scores below 0.2 indicated severe transcription errors or instances where the instrumental part, rather than singing\n552\tmelodies, was transcribed.\n553\tSecond, through a manual inspection of around 150 clips as a\n554\tquality check, we did not find any that were assigned the incorrect\n555\toctave with confidence above 0.2. As a result, we obtain the clips\n556\twith only singing vocals with correct absolute octave information,\n557\tresulting in 9,468 clips that are ready to be converted as **kern\n558\tscores in the following step.\n559\t\n560\t2.3\n561\t\n562\t**kern Conversion\n563\t\n564\tThe original SheetSage files are in JSON format. To use the files\n565\tin our A2S pipeline, we convert them to the **kern9 format. This\n566\tconversion process is illustrated in Figure 3 and based upon four\n567\t9 https://www.humdrum.org/rep/kern/\n568\t\n569\t\f10 In music theory, enharmonic refers to two or more distinct note spellings (e.g. note\n570\t\n571\tnames with different accidentals) that correspond to the same exact pitch class in\n572\ttwelve-tone equal temperament. For example, C♯ and D♭ are enharmonic equivalents: they sound identical, but are written differently in the score and serve different\n573\tharmonic and contextual functions in a piece of music.\n574\t11 https://www.hooktheory.com/theorytab, HookTheory is the Internet’s largest database of songs broken down by music theory, which is also the source of the original\n575\tSheetSage annotations.\n576\t\n577\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n578\t\n579\t(a) Clip Duration\n580\t2000\n581\t0\n582\t\n583\t0\n584\t\n585\t20\n586\t\n587\t40\n588\t\n589\t60\n590\t\n591\tSeconds\n592\t\n593\t80\n594\t\n595\t100\n596\t\n597\tNumber of clips\n598\t\n599\tmain steps: converting the melody notes, converting the chord\n600\tinformation, aligning and quantising the rhythm of the melody and\n601\tchords, and finally merging everything in a Humdrum **kern file.\n602\t(1) Melody: The original JSON file represents the pitch class\n603\tof each melodic note using an integer between 0 and 11, but this\n604\trepresentation is enharmonic10 and does not specify note spelling,\n605\twhich is essential for standard music score generation. From the\n606\tperspective of music theory, note spellings should be consistent\n607\twith the underlying key signature [27].\n608\tWe therefore extract the key signature information from the\n609\tsource JSON files, and derive the note spellings from pitch classes\n610\taccordingly.\n611\t(2) Chords: Since the SheetSage-A2S dataset represents lead\n612\tsheets, chords are annotated in addition to the melody. Chords\n613\tare defined by root pitch class, interval content in semitones, and\n614\tinversion. Root note spelling is obtained in the same way as melody\n615\tnotes if it belongs to the scale. For out-of-scale notes, enharmonic\n616\tspelling is chosen by matching the key’s alteration type (e.g. using\n617\tsharps in a key with sharps like D Major). Other tones spelling is\n618\tconducted with respect to the root note, matching the intervallic\n619\tcontent of the chord to a dictionary of known chord natures to\n620\tchoose the correct enharmonic spelling.\n621\tThe final chord labelling step produces a label in Harte syntax\n622\t[12] of the form <root>:<nature>[/bass], such as A:min7/3 for\n623\tan A minor seventh chord in first inversion, for example.\n624\tThe <bass> component of the chord label is omitted if it is identical to the root note, and the <nature> is obtained from matching\n625\tthe intervallic content to a pre-existing chord database that was\n626\tassembled as needed while preparing the dataset.\n627\t(3) Temporal Alignment and Rhythmic Quantisation: The melody\n628\tnotes and chord annotations are encoded independently, each carrying its own beat-indexed onset and offset values. For temporal\n629\talignment, we merge all note and chord events, then iterate over\n630\tthe resulting time slices. At each slice, we build the **kern and\n631\t**cdata spines by writing the melody token and the current chord\n632\tsymbol as a tab-separated row, with a dot (.) filling any column\n633\twhere no new event begins. We then conduct rhythm quantisation\n634\tby mapping the duration values to **kern rhythm tokens, using\n635\trational fractions with a maximum denominator of 96 to deal with\n636\tfast grace notes. For example, durations of 1 and 0.5 (a quarter note\n637\tand an eighth note) in the JSON file becomes respectively 4 and 8\n638\tin **kern.\n639\t(4) Symbolic Score: We finish the conversion by merging the\n640\ttwo musical spines **kern (for the melody) and **cdata (for the\n641\tchords) with a header and a footer. The header declares global\n642\tattributes including clef, key and time signatures, as well as artist\n643\tand clip metadata.\n644\tThe footer appends bibliographical records including the original\n645\tHookTheory11 and YouTube links.\n646\t\n647\tNumber of clips\n648\t\n649\tAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n650\t\n651\t(b) Inferred Octave\n652\t\n653\t5000\n654\t0\n655\t\n656\t2\n657\t\n658\t3\n659\t\n660\t4\n661\t\n662\tOctave\n663\t\n664\t5\n665\t\n666\tFigure 4: SheetSage-A2S dataset statistics. (a) Clip duration,\n667\ttruncated at 100 seconds; (b) Inferred absolute octave.\n668\t\n669\t2.4\n670\t\n671\tData Statistics\n672\t\n673\tThe 9,468 clips (Figure 4) have a mean duration of 23.36 seconds\n674\twith a standard deviation of ∼11.34 seconds; 12 clips exceed 100\n675\tseconds.\n676\tThe clips originate from 6,066 unique songs across 2,891 artists.\n677\tWhile 3,635 songs are represented by a single clip, the remainder\n678\tcontribute several. The songs per artist distribution is long-tailed:\n679\t2,005 artists contribute only one song, with an overall mean of 2.1\n680\tsongs per artist. A notable outlier is The Beatles, with 127 songs.\n681\tUsing the octave inference process introduced in\n682\tSection 2.2 ,\n683\t98.66% of clips are assigned to octave three or four.\n684\t\n685\t3\n686\t\n687\tProposed Method\n688\t\n689\tThis section describes our proposed A2S method. We first detail\n690\tthe core model architecture, including the baseline design and our\n691\tenhancements leveraging a pre-trained audio foundation model.\n692\tWe then discuss the\n693\tdata augmentation techniques implemented.\n694\t\n695\t3.1\n696\t\n697\tModel Configuration\n698\t\n699\tBaseline Architecture. We start with the model from [1] shown\n700\tin Figure 5(a). This model is a CNN encoder with a Transformer\n701\tdecoder. The CNN encoder first turns an input spectrogram into a\n702\t2D feature map. After adding 2D positional encodings, the feature\n703\tmap is flattened to form the cross-attention memory passed to the\n704\tTransformer decoder.\n705\tProposed Architecture. Our newly-proposed A2S model is illustrated in Figure 5(b). To establish a stronger foundation, we first\n706\tincrease the capacity of the Transformer decoder by expanding the\n707\tTransformer decoder’s feedforward dimension from 256 to 1024\n708\tand changing its normalisation order from post-norm to pre-norm\n709\tin order to improve training stability [35]. We refer to this change as\n710\tthe 1024-PreNorm baseline, which will be further studied in\n711\tSection 5.1 . Then, we adopt the MuQ pretrained model [38] to replace\n712\tthe vanilla CNN encoder as a feature extractor from audio files,\n713\tto study whether it can mitigate data scarcity issues discussed in\n714\tSection 1 . We utilise MuQ due to its strong overall performance\n715\ton the music understanding benchmark MARBLE [36], suggesting\n716\tthat it learns useful representations for downstream music tasks.\n717\tWe specially keep MuQ frozen and use its final layer’s hiddenstate sequence as output. We then use a linear projection followed\n718\tby LayerNorm to reduce the MuQ embedding size from its original\n719\t1024 to 256.\n720\tAs MuQ outputs a one-dimensional sequence, we use a onedimensional sinusoidal positional encoding in place of the baseline’s\n721\t2D one. Finally, the resulting features (note that MuQ outputs 25\n722\tfeature vectors per second, compared to ∼5.38 per second from\n723\t\n724\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n725\t\n726\tEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n727\t\n728\ta 7× expansion of the training set. We use Rubber Band12 for both\n729\tpitch shifting and time stretching.\n730\t\n731\t(a) Previous baseline\n732\t2D Positional\n733\tEncoding\n734\t\n735\tAudio Signal\n736\tCNN\n737\tEncoder\n738\t\n739\tFlatten\n740\t\n741\t4\n742\t\n743\tSpectrogram\n744\tNorm\n745\tAdd\n746\t256D FFN\n747\t\n748\t1D\n749\tPositional\n750\tEncoding\n751\t\n752\tNorm\n753\tAdd\n754\tCross Attention\n755\t\n756\t256D\n757\tEmbedding\n758\t\n759\tNorm\n760\t\n761\tAdd\n762\tSelf Attention\n763\t\n764\tPreviously\n765\tpredicted tokens\n766\t\n767\tOutput\n768\tProbabilities\n769\tNotation\n770\tProjection\n771\t\n772\t4.1\n773\t(b) Our proposed model\n774\t1D Positional\n775\tEncoding\n776\tMuQ\n777\t(Frozen)\n778\t\n779\tLinear\n780\tProjection +\n781\tLayerNorm\n782\t\n783\tAdd\n784\t1024D FFN\n785\tNorm\n786\t\n787\t1D\n788\tPositional\n789\tEncoding\n790\t\n791\tAdd\n792\t\n793\t256D\n794\tEmbedding\n795\t\n796\tAdd\n797\tCross Attention\n798\tNorm\n799\t\n800\tSelf Attention\n801\tNorm\n802\t\n803\tPreviously\n804\tpredicted tokens\n805\t\n806\tOutput\n807\tProbabilities\n808\tNotation\n809\tProjection\n810\t\n811\t8x Transformer Decoder Layer\n812\t\n813\tArgmax\n814\t\n815\tFigure 5: Comparison of our proposed architecture against\n816\tthe baseline of [1]. Both models use an autoregressive Transformer decoder to generate **kern score symbols. We replace\n817\tthe CNN encoder from the baseline with MuQ [38], a pretrained model to extract audio features, followed by a linear projection and LayerNorm to map MuQ’s embedding\n818\tdimension from 1024 to 256. We also increase the decoder\n819\tfeedforward dimension from 256 to 1024, and change its normalisation order from post-norm to pre-norm.\n820\t\n821\tthe baseline CNN encoder) are then input as the cross-attention\n822\tmemory to the Transformer decoder.\n823\t\n824\t3.2\n825\t\n826\tWe conduct experiments on two datasets: the Quartets collection\n827\tand our new SheetSage-A2S dataset. We provide implementation\n828\tdetails for training and testing the baseline model and our proposed\n829\tarchitecture.\n830\t\n831\t8x Transformer Decoder Layer\n832\t\n833\tArgmax\n834\t\n835\tAudio Signal\n836\t\n837\tExperiments\n838\t\n839\tData Augmentation\n840\t\n841\tTo improve robustness and increase the amount of training data,\n842\twe apply offline data augmentation to the training split. We implement pitch-shifting and time-stretching, leaving the exploration of\n843\tother augmentation techniques to future work. We denote pitch\n844\tshifting by P𝑘 , where 𝑘 is measured in semitones, and time stretching by T𝑠 , where 𝑠 is the stretch factor. For each training pair\n845\t(𝑥, 𝑦), we generate six augmented variants for each pitch shift\n846\t𝑘 ∈ {−3, −2, −1, 1, 2, 3}. For each such variant, we independently\n847\tsample a time-stretch factor 𝑠 ∈ {0.9, 0.95, 1.05, 1.1} and construct\n848\taugmented audio 𝑥 ′ = T𝑠 (P𝑘 (𝑥)). These ranges were chosen to\n849\tprovide the model with enough variety to learn to handle different\n850\tfrequency ranges and tempos while ensuring the augmented audio\n851\tstill sounds like a real performance.\n852\tThe symbolic target is transposed accordingly, giving 𝑦 ′ = P𝑘 (𝑦).\n853\tWe do not modify 𝑦 for T𝑠 on the SheetSage-A2S dataset. As the\n854\ttarget representation uses relative note values and lacks an explicit\n855\tmeter, it is tempo-invariant. However, for the Quartets dataset,\n856\tthe tempo metadata is updated accordingly. The original training\n857\texample is retained alongside its augmented variants, resulting in\n858\t\n859\tConfigurations\n860\t\n861\tQuartets Dataset. We evaluate our approach on the Quartets collection, which comprises string quartets by Mozart, Beethoven, and\n862\tHaydn, serving as a direct point of comparison with [1]. The dataset\n863\tis partitioned into 70% training, 15% validation, and 15% test sets,\n864\tconsistent with [1]. For our experiments on this dataset, we train\n865\ta baseline A2S model using the original word-level tokenisation.\n866\tWe then incrementally incorporate MuQ, the pre-trained encoder\n867\tand our data augmentation strategy to evaluate their individual\n868\tcontributions.\n869\tSheetSage-A2S Dataset. Following the methodology in [10], this\n870\tdataset employs an artist-stratified split of 80% training, 10% validation, and 10% testing.\n871\tFor both datasets, the audio files were resampled to 22,050 Hz\n872\twhen using the baseline architecture, following [1], and to 24,000\n873\tHz when using MuQ [38]. In both cases, we first train a baseline\n874\tA2S model. We then evaluate the incremental improvements from\n875\tincorporating MuQ and our data augmentation strategy.\n876\t\n877\t4.2\n878\t\n879\tImplementation Details\n880\t\n881\tEvaluation. Symbol Error Rate (SER) computes the Levenshtein\n882\tdistance between ground truth and prediction: the minimum number of single-element edits (insertions, deletions, or substitutions)\n883\trequired to transform the predicted sequence into the target sequence. We use SER as our primary metric for both datasets, with\n884\tall sequences generated using greedy decoding at inference time.\n885\tThis ensures direct compatibility with existing A2S work [1], and\n886\talso provides a consistent baseline against which future models\n887\ttrained on SheetSage-A2S can be evaluated. While MV2H [22] has\n888\tbeen used in other audio-to-score work, current implementations\n889\tdo not support the lead sheet format we generated in Section 2.3.\n890\tWe therefore provide both a per-spine SER breakdown and SER\n891\tscores for both melody and chord instead.\n892\tTraining. Models are trained using the AdamW optimiser with a\n893\tweight decay of 1e-3, a constant learning rate of 5e-5, and a batch\n894\tsize of 8. This contrasts with the original training setup (Adam, no\n895\tweight decay, lr 1e-4, batch size 1).\n896\tWe made this switch as we found through empirical testing that\n897\tit improved training stability. We use an early-stopping patience of\n898\t5 epochs on the validation loss with a minimum delta of 1e-3, and\n899\tretain the best model.\n900\tOn both datasets the model is trained with cross-entropy loss.\n901\tAs the SheetSage-A2S annotations are user-generated and contain\n902\toccasional errors, we apply label smoothing of 0.1 while training on\n903\tSheetSage-A2S, which softens the target distribution and reduces\n904\t12 https://breakfastquay.com/rubberband/\n905\t\n906\t\fAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n907\t\n908\tTable 2: Symbol error rate (SER) for each proposed modification, with a granular breakdown (melody and chord SERs)\n909\ton SheetSage-A2S. Each row adds one component to the baseline of [1]. The spine-level metrics exclude structural tokens\n910\t(tabs and newlines), which are comparatively easy to predict,\n911\tand are therefore expected to be higher than the overall SER.\n912\tMelody and chord SERs are not provided for the Quartets\n913\tdataset, as it doesn’t contain chord annotations, and melodic\n914\tcontent can span multiple distinct spines.\n915\t\n916\tVariant\n917\t\n918\tSER\n919\t\n920\tBaseline [1]\n921\t+1024-Pre-Norm\n922\t+MuQ encoder\n923\t+Augmented data\n924\t\n925\t66.85\n926\t53.7\n927\t25.39\n928\t20.92\n929\t\n930\tSheetSage-A2S\n931\tMelody SER Chord SER\n932\t106.88\n933\t90.56\n934\t46.21\n935\t38.62\n936\t\n937\t64.12\n938\t51.8\n939\t26.76\n940\t22.28\n941\t\n942\tQuartets\n943\tSER\n944\t15.3\n945\t8.48\n946\t7.16\n947\t4.98\n948\t\n949\tthe penalty for deviating from potentially incorrect ground truth\n950\tlabels. We found this label smoothing provided a small SER decrease.\n951\t\n952\tSER on SheetSage-A2S. While songs in SheetSage-A2S may overlap with the MSD, quantifying this is non-trivial due to naming\n953\tinconsistencies across the datasets. However, the consistent gains\n954\tfrom MuQ on the stylistically distinct Quartets corpus suggest the\n955\timprovements stem from generally useful learned representations.\n956\tTokenisation. To determine the most effective tokenisation strategy, we conducted an ablation study comparing the three methods\n957\tproposed by [20]: word-level, mid-level, and character-level tokenisation. All experiments were run using our MuQ architecture prior\n958\tto applying data augmentation. As shown in Table 3, word-level\n959\ttokenisation outperformed both alternatives. Although mid-level tokenisation performed best in [20] and offers theoretical advantages\n960\tsuch as a bijective mapping between graphical symbols and tokens,\n961\tand a reduced vocabulary size, these advantages did not translate\n962\tto our dataset. We partly attribute this to the long sequence lengths\n963\tpresent in the SheetSage-A2S dataset, where the reduced sequence\n964\tlength produced by word-level tokenisation seems more beneficial.\n965\tTable 3: Performances of different tokenisation methods\n966\ton the SheetSage-A2S dataset. The model used is the MuQ\n967\tvariant without augmentation.\n968\t\n969\t5 Results\n970\t5.1 Transcription Performance\n971\tThe transcription performance on both datasets is detailed in Table 2, using the word-level tokenisation scheme identical to the\n972\tbaseline model [1]. On Quartets, our proposed model achieves an\n973\tSER of 4.98, representing a 67.5% relative reduction from the baseline SER of 15.3.\n974\tFor a more granular and musically meaningful evaluation, we\n975\talso include the SER per spine, namely melody and chord SER\n976\tresults for our SheetSage-A2S dataset in Table 2. Every proposed\n977\tmodification improves all of these scores. Note that per-spine SER\n978\tis computed excluding structural tokens (such as tab and newline),\n979\twhich are relatively easier to predict, so per-spine SER values are\n980\texpected to be higher than normal SER. SER can also exceed 100%.\n981\t\n982\t5.2\n983\t\n984\tAblation study\n985\t\n986\tEncoder and augmentation. To evaluate the individual contributions\n987\tof the proposed methods, in Table 2 we analyse the performance of\n988\tintermediate models bridging the baseline [1] and the final architecture. The evaluation isolates three primary modifications: the\n989\tmodel architecture finetuning, the integration of the MuQ feature\n990\textractor, and the application of data augmentation.\n991\tFirst, our 1024-PreNorm baseline (see\n992\tSection 3.1 ) achieves\n993\tan SER of 8.48 on the Quartets dataset and 53.7 on SheetSage-A2S.\n994\tBased on our preliminary experiments, we primarily attribute the\n995\timprovement of the original model’s 15.3 SER [1] on Quartets and\n996\t66.85 on SheetSage-A2S to the increased feedforward dimension\n997\tfrom 256 to 1024. Next, replacing the vanilla CNN encoder with\n998\tfrozen MuQ features further reduces the Quartets SER to 7.16. More\n999\tnotably, for SheetSage-A2S, adding MuQ decreases the SER to 25.39.\n1000\tThe larger performance gain on SheetSage-A2S is expected, as the\n1001\tMuQ model we utilise was pre-trained on the Million Song Dataset\n1002\t(MSD), a large corpus of popular music. Finally, applying data augmentation yields our best results: a 4.98 SER on Quartets and 20.92\n1003\t\n1004\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1005\t\n1006\tMethod\n1007\tCharacter level\n1008\tMid level\n1009\tWord level\n1010\t\n1011\t6\n1012\t\n1013\tSymbol Error Rate\n1014\t33.97\n1015\t30.44\n1016\t25.39\n1017\t\n1018\tToken count\n1019\t61\n1020\t224\n1021\t4763\n1022\t\n1023\tDiscussion\n1024\t\n1025\tIn this section, we discuss some limitations observed from using the\n1026\tSheetSage-A2S dataset, in particular due to the non-standardised\n1027\tnature of the annotations. We then propose a qualitative analysis\n1028\tof a transcription result from our best performing model to reflect\n1029\tupon the strengths and weaknesses of our approach.\n1030\t\n1031\t6.1\n1032\t\n1033\tUser-Annotation Variabilities\n1034\t\n1035\tA contributing factor to the higher SER on the SheetSage-A2S\n1036\tdataset is the inherent variability of user-generated content. Transcribers slightly differ in subtle stylistic choices, which the model\n1037\thas no way of discerning from the audio. We choose to release\n1038\tthe core dataset with minimal preprocessing. However, this lack of\n1039\tstandardisation is likely to introduce performance penalties.\n1040\tFirst, users transcribe melodies and chords with varying standards. Some annotators label a chord only once when the harmony\n1041\tchanges, while others annotate the same chord repeatedly for every\n1042\trhythmic strum, or once per bar. Furthermore, we observe issues\n1043\twith inconsistent transcriptions, where some users stop transcribing before the audio clip officially ends as the remaining (usually\n1044\tsmall) part does not belong to the current music section, which\n1045\tpenalises the model for correctly transcribing the remaining audio.\n1046\tSome users also transcribe small parts of the accompaniment when\n1047\tvocals are silent, a behaviour our model did not catch on as it is not\n1048\tprevalent in the training data.\n1049\tSecond, melodies expect different levels of rhythmic granularity.\n1050\tThis is partly due to user annotation inconsistencies: some users\n1051\tannotate separate notes for every word, some for every syllable,\n1052\t\n1053\t\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1054\t\n1055\tEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n1056\t\n1057\tissues are especially common when the same pitch is repeated\n1058\tmultiple times in succession (common for example, in rap verse\n1059\tannotations). Furthermore, the model can misidentify note offsets,\n1060\teither cutting a note short to insert an incorrect rest, or holding\n1061\ta note slightly too long through a space where a brief rest should\n1062\toccur. This error is shown in Figure 6 in green, where in the second measure the model terminates the half note prematurely by\n1063\tinserting an incorrect rest.\n1064\tUltimately, while this internal reshuffling causes the exact start\n1065\tand end times of individual notes to shift, the overall phrase length\n1066\tremains intact, and these timing deviations rarely exceed an eighth\n1067\tnote. Musically, these offset errors are minor issues as even human\n1068\ttranscribers might disagree on smooth offsets like those of singing\n1069\tvocals. Other than that, the model excels at predicting the essential\n1070\tmusical elements, including pitches, note onsets, chord root, and\n1071\tthe overall musical structure, which align well with the original\n1072\taudio.\n1073\t\n1074\t7\n1075\tFigure 6: Measures 5 to 7 from Love Live - Bokutachi wa Hitotsu no Hikari.12 It demonstrates that our best model can\n1076\thave trouble determining exact note-rest boundaries and\n1077\tnon-triad chord labels.\n1078\t\n1079\tand others only annotate when the pitch changes. Furthermore,\n1080\tsingers often employ expressive vocal techniques, such as subtle\n1081\tpitch bends or grace notes. Some users annotate both the start\n1082\tand end of the bend, while others only annotate the core note. All\n1083\tthe aforementioned cases serve as inconsistencies in the training\n1084\tand evaluation processes that compromise the performances of our\n1085\tmodel on the SheetSage-A2S dataset.\n1086\t\n1087\t6.2\n1088\t\n1089\tQualitative analysis\n1090\t\n1091\tTo further illustrate the model’s behaviour on the SheetSage-A2S\n1092\tdataset, we present a qualitative analysis of the transcription results\n1093\tgenerated by our best-performing model (MuQ variant with data\n1094\taugmentation), along with the ground truth shown in Figure 6.\n1095\tFor chord transcription, the model typically identifies root notes\n1096\tcorrectly but has difficulty labelling chords beyond simple triads.\n1097\tAdditionally, the model can sporadically label the inversion of an\n1098\tinverted chord as the root of the chord. Finally, in cases where a\n1099\tsong maintains the same root but cycles through subtly different\n1100\tchord qualities, the model tends to sustain the quality of its initial\n1101\tchord prediction without registering the subsequent changes. In\n1102\tFigure 6, for instance, the last Eb:sus4 is not identified and the\n1103\tEb:11 is maintained. The disambiguation between these chords\n1104\tcan however be subtle as both contain a perfect fourth (called\n1105\t11th when an octave higher) as a characteristic tone. Overall, the\n1106\tmodel successfully captures the overarching chord progression but\n1107\tsimplifies the first F:min7 chord to a F:min.\n1108\tThe model’s most significant difficulty lies in predicting the exact\n1109\tnote durations, indicated as Timing Issue in Figure 6.\n1110\tTwo examples are note consolidation (merging consecutive notes)\n1111\tand note separation (splitting a single note). We observed that these\n1112\t12 https://hookpad.hooktheory.com/?idOfSong=yvmrlMLVxOW - measures 5-7\n1113\t\n1114\tConclusion and Future Work\n1115\t\n1116\tIn this paper, we first introduce the new SheetSage-A2S Dataset, a\n1117\tdedicated lead sheet corpus for supporting A2S research in the style\n1118\tof popular music. It contains 61 hours of audio for 6,066 unique\n1119\tsongs, paired with **kern lead sheet annotations. We improve\n1120\texisting A2S frameworks by using data augmentation and MuQ [38],\n1121\ta pre-trained music feature extractor, to enhance generalisation\n1122\tand capture high-quality semantic audio features. Experiments\n1123\tshow our A2S system significantly outperforms the existing stateof-the-art [1] for classical music, and achieves 20.92% SER on the\n1124\tSheetSage-A2S dataset for popular music, setting a competitive\n1125\tbaseline for future research.\n1126\tLooking ahead, we will expand the SheetSage-A2S dataset with\n1127\tinstrument-performed melody clips, as current octave inference\n1128\tonly retains clips with singing vocals.\n1129\tWhile this work preserves the users’ annotations with minimal\n1130\tpre-processing to present the SheetSage-A2S dataset in its original\n1131\tform, we plan to resolve the annotation variabilities and inconsistencies for the better training and evaluation of A2S models. We\n1132\twill also implement more advanced evaluation metrics, to better\n1133\tqualify the mistakes made by our model and their musical significance. Lastly, we plan to carry out an inter-annotator study to\n1134\tquantify the difference in annotation styles between annotations\n1135\tfor our clips.\n1136\t\n1137\tAcknowledgments\n1138\tThe computational resources are supported by SongShan Lake HPC\n1139\tCenter (SSL-HPC) in Great Bay University.\n1140\t\n1141\t\fAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n1142\t\n1143\tReferences\n1144\t[1] María Alfaro-Contreras, Antonio Ríos-Vila, Jose J. Valero-Mas, and Jorge CalvoZaragoza. 2024. A Transformer Approach for Polyphonic Audio-to-Score Transcription. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\n1145\tand Signal Processing (ICASSP). 706–710.\n1146\t[2] Victor Arroyo, Jose J. Valero-Mas, Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza, and Antonio Pertusa.\n1147\t2022. Neural Audio-To-Score Music Transcription For Unconstrained Polyphony\n1148\tUsing Compact Output Representations. In Proceedings of the IEEE International\n1149\tConference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). 4603–4607.\n1150\t[3] Emmanouil Benetos, Simon Dixon, Zhiyao Duan, and Sebastian Ewert. 2019.\n1151\tAutomatic Music Transcription: An Overview. IEEE Signal Processing Magazine\n1152\t36, 1 (2019), 20–30.\n1153\t[4] Tim Beyer and Angela Dai. 2024. End-to-End Piano Performance-MIDI to Score\n1154\tConversion with Transformers. In Proceedings of the 25th International Society\n1155\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 319–326.\n1156\t[5] Sebastian Böck, Filip Korzeniowski, Jan Schlüter, Florian Krebs, and Gerhard\n1157\tWidmer. 2016. madmom: A New Python Audio and Music Signal Processing\n1158\tLibrary. In Proceedings of the 24th ACM International Conference on Multimedia.\n1159\t1174–1178.\n1160\t[6] Sebastian Böck, Florian Krebs, and Gerhard Widmer. 2016. Joint Beat and Downbeat Tracking with Recurrent Neural Networks. In Proceedings of the 17th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 255–261.\n1161\t[7] Barry S Brook. 1965. The simplified\" plaine and easie code system\" for notating\n1162\tmusic: a proposal for international adoption. Fontes artis musicae 12, 2/3 (1965),\n1163\t156–160.\n1164\t[8] Ralf Gunter Correa Carvalho and Paris Smaragdis. 2017. Towards End-to-end\n1165\tPolyphonic Music Transcription: Transforming Music Audio Directly to a Score.\n1166\tIn IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to Audio and Acoustics\n1167\t(WASPAA). 151–155.\n1168\t[9] Andrea Cogliati, David Temperley, and Zhiyao Duan. 2016. Transcribing Human\n1169\tPiano Performances into Music Notation. In Proceedings of the 17th International\n1170\tSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 758–764.\n1171\t[10] Chris Donahue, John Thickstun, and Percy Liang. 2022. Melody Transcription\n1172\tVia Generative Pre-Training. In Proceedings of the 23rd International Society for\n1173\tMusic Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 485–492.\n1174\t[11] Drew Edwards, Simon Dixon, Emmanouil Benetos, Akira Maezawa, and Yuta\n1175\tKusaka. 2024. A Data-Driven Analysis of Robust Automatic Piano Transcription.\n1176\tIEEE Signal Processing Letters 31 (2024), 681–685.\n1177\t[12] Christopher Harte, Mark Sandler, Samer Abdallah, and Emilia Gomez. 2005. Symbolic Representation of Musical Chords: A Proposed Syntax for Text Annotations.\n1178\tIn Proceedings of the 6th International Society for Music Information Retrieval\n1179\tConference (ISMIR). 66–71.\n1180\t[13] Celine Jacques and Axel Roebel. 2019. Data Augmentation for Drum Transcription\n1181\twith Convolutional Neural Networks. In Proceedings of the 27th European Signal\n1182\tProcessing Conference (EUSIPCO). 1–5.\n1183\t[14] Fatemeh Jamshidi, Gary Pike, Amit Das, and Richard Chapman. 2024. Machine\n1184\tLearning Techniques in Automatic Music Transcription: A Systematic Survey. https:\n1185\t//arxiv.org/abs/2406.15249 A Preprint.\n1186\t[15] Jong Wook Kim, Justin Salamon, Peter Li, and Juan Pablo Bello. 2018. CREPE:\n1187\tA Convolutional Representation for Pitch Estimation. In Proceedings of the IEEE\n1188\tInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP).\n1189\t161–165.\n1190\t[16] Yizhi Li, Ruibin Yuan, Ge Zhang, Yinghao Ma, Xingran Chen, Hanzhi Yin, Chenghao Xiao, Chenghua Lin, Anton Ragni, Emmanouil Benetos, Norbert Gyenge,\n1191\tRoger Dannenberg, Ruibo Liu, Wenhu Chen, Gus Xia, Yemin Shi, Wenhao Huang,\n1192\tZili Wang, Yike Guo, and Jie Fu. 2024. MERT: Acoustic Music Understanding\n1193\tModel with Large-Scale Self-supervised Training. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR). 1–24.\n1194\t[17] Lele Liu and Emmanouil Benetos. 2021. From Audio to Music Notation. In\n1195\tHandbook of Artificial Intelligence for Music: Foundations, Advanced Approaches,\n1196\tand Developments for Creativity. Springer International Publishing, 693–714.\n1197\t[18] Lele Liu, Veronica Morfi, and Emmanouil Benetos. 2021. Joint Multi-Pitch Detection and Score Transcription for Polyphonic Piano Music. In Proceedings of the\n1198\tIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP).\n1199\t281–285.\n1200\t[19] Yinghao Ma, Anders Øland, Anton Ragni, Bleiz MacSen Del Sette, Charalampos\n1201\tSaitis, Chris Donahue, Chenghua Lin, Christos Plachouras, Emmanouil Benetos, Elona Shatri, Fabio Morreale, Ge Zhang, György Fazekas, Gus Xia, Huan\n1202\tZhang, Ilaria Manco, Jiawen Huang, Julien Guinot, Liwei Lin, Luca Marinelli, Max\n1203\tW. Y. Lam, Megha Sharma, Qiuqiang Kong, Roger B. Dannenberg, Ruibin Yuan,\n1204\tShangda Wu, Shih-Lun Wu, Shuqi Dai, Shun Lei, Shiyin Kang, Simon Dixon,\n1205\t\n1206\tMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n1207\t\n1208\tWenhu Chen, Wenhao Huang, Xingjian Du, Xingwei Qu, Xu Tan, Yizhi Li, Zeyue\n1209\tTian, Zhiyong Wu, Zhizheng Wu, Ziyang Ma, and Ziyu Wang. 2024. Foundation\n1210\tModels for Music: A Survey. http://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14340 A Preprint.\n1211\t[20] Juan C. Martinez-Sevilla, Francesco Foscarin, Patricia Garcia-Iasci, David Rizo,\n1212\tJorge Calvo-Zaragoza, and Gerhard Widmer. 2025. Optical Music Recognition\n1213\tof Jazz Lead Sheets. In Proceedings of the 26th International Society for Music\n1214\tInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 696–702.\n1215\t[21] Brian McFee, Eric J Humphrey, and Juan P Bello. 2015. A Software Framework\n1216\tfor Musical Data Augmentation. In Proceedings of the 16th International Society\n1217\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 248–254.\n1218\t[22] Andrew McLeod and Mark Steedman. 2018. Evaluating Automatic Polyphonic\n1219\tMusic Transcription. In Proceedings of the 19th International Society for Music\n1220\tInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 42–49.\n1221\t[23] Xavier Riley, Drew Edwards, and Simon Dixon. 2024. High Resolution Guitar\n1222\tTranscription Via Domain Adaptation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International\n1223\tConference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). 1051–1055.\n1224\t[24] Miguel A Román, Antonio Pertusa, and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza. 2018. An End-toEnd Framework for Audio-to-Score Music Transcription on Monophonic Excerpts.\n1225\tIn Proceedings of the 19th International Society for Music Information Retrieval\n1226\tConference (ISMIR). 34–41.\n1227\t[25] Miguel A Román, Antonio Pertusa, and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza. 2019. A Holistic\n1228\tApproach to Polyphonic Music Transcription with Neural Networks. In Proceedings of the 20th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference\n1229\t(ISMIR). 731–737.\n1230\t[26] Miguel A. Román, Antonio Pertusa, and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza. 2020. Data\n1231\tRepresentations for Audio-to-Score Monophonic Music Transcription. Expert\n1232\tSystems with Applications 162 (2020), 113769.\n1233\t[27] Julian Rushton. 2001. Enharmonic. Grove Music Online, Vol. 1. Oxford University\n1234\tPress.\n1235\t[28] Craig Stuart Sapp. 2005. Online Database of Scores in the Humdrum File Format.\n1236\tIn Proceedings of the 6th International Society for Music Information Retrieval\n1237\tConference (ISMIR). 664–665.\n1238\t[29] John Thickstun, Zaid Harchaoui, Dean P. Foster, and Sham M. Kakade. 2018.\n1239\tInvariances and data augmentation for supervised music transcription. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\n1240\tProcessing (ICASSP). 2241–2245.\n1241\t[30] Ju-Chiang Wang, Wei-Tsung Lu, and Jitong Chen. 2024. Mel-RoFormer for\n1242\tVocal Separation and Vocal Melody Transcription. In Proceedings of the 25th\n1243\tInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 454–461.\n1244\t[31] Haojie Wei, Xueke Cao, Tangpeng Dan, and Yueguo Chen. 2023. RMVPE: A\n1245\trobust model for vocal pitch estimation in polyphonic music. In Proceedings of\n1246\tthe 24th Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association\n1247\t(Interspeech). 5421–5425.\n1248\t[32] Weixing Wei, Peilin Li, Yi Yu, and Wei Li. 2022. HarmoF0: Logarithmic scale\n1249\tdilated convolution for pitch estimation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International\n1250\tConference on Multimedia and Expo (ICME). 1–6.\n1251\t[33] Minz Won, Yun-Ning Hung, and Duc Le. 2024. A Foundation Model for Music\n1252\tInformatics. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\n1253\tSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). 1226–1230.\n1254\t[34] Chih-Wei Wu and Alexander Lerch. 2017. Automatic Drum Transcription Using\n1255\tthe Student-Teacher Learning Paradigm with Unlabeled Music Data. In Proceedings of the 18th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference\n1256\t(ISMIR). 613–620.\n1257\t[35] Ruibin Xiong, Yunchang Yang, Di He, Kai Zheng, Shuxin Zheng, Chen Xing,\n1258\tHuishuai Zhang, Yanyan Lan, Liwei Wang, and Tieyan Liu. 2020. On Layer\n1259\tNormalization in the Transformer Architecture. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Machine Learning. 10524–10533.\n1260\t[36] Ruibin Yuan, Yinghao Ma, Yizhi Li, Ge Zhang, Xingran Chen, Hanzhi Yin, zhuo le,\n1261\tYiqi Liu, Jiawen Huang, Zeyue Tian, Binyue Deng, Ningzhi Wang, Chenghua Lin,\n1262\tEmmanouil Benetos, Anton Ragni, Norbert Gyenge, Roger Dannenberg, Wenhu\n1263\tChen, Gus Xia, Wei Xue, Si Liu, Shi Wang, Ruibo Liu, Yike Guo, and Jie Fu. 2023.\n1264\tMARBLE: Music audio representation benchmark for universal evaluation. In\n1265\tProceedings of the Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems\n1266\t(NeurIPS). 39626–39647.\n1267\t[37] Chen Zhang, Jiaxing Yu, LuChin Chang, Xu Tan, Jiawei Chen, Tao Qin, and Kejun\n1268\tZhang. 2022. PDAugment: Data Augmentation by Pitch and Duration Adjustments for Automatic Lyrics Transcription. In Proceedings of the 23rd International\n1269\tSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 454–461.\n1270\t[38] Haina Zhu, Yizhi Zhou, Hangting Chen, Jianwei Yu, Ziyang Ma, Rongzhi Gu, Yi\n1271\tLuo, Wei Tan, and Xie Chen. 2025. MuQ: Self-Supervised Music Representation\n1272\tLearning With Mel Residual Vector Quantization. IEEE Transactions on Audio,\n1273\tSpeech and Language Processing 33 (2025), 3653–3664.\n1274\t\n1275\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "ce86571f-08ce-4b2c-8f9e-59df2c73886e", "uuid": "829d2031-1ca4-4d6a-9ae7-97c72048ae0a", "timestamp": "2026-08-07T06:20:51.014Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/a2s.txt", "content": "Audio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data\nAugmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\nEoin Cummins∗\n\nZhongyi Huang∗\n\nAlexandre D’Hooge\n\nUniversity College Dublin\nDublin, Ireland\n\nGuangxi Normal University\nGuilin, China\n\nGreat Bay University\nDongguan, Guangdong, China\n\nZhuoro Mo∗\n\nYaolong Ju†\n\nShenzhen University\nShenzhen, China\n\nGreat Bay University\nDongguan, Guangdong, China\njuyaolong@gbu.edu.cn\n\narXiv:2608.06165v1 [cs.SD] 6 Aug 2026\n\nAbstract\nExisting audio-to-score (A2S) systems primarily focus on classical\nmusic, and the application to popular music remains underexplored.\nThis paper first presents the new SheetSage-A2S Dataset, which\nincludes 61 hours of audio with **kern score encodings for 9,468\nclips originating from 6,066 unique songs, the first of its kind to\nfacilitate A2S research for popular music. Additionally, we improve\non existing A2S approaches by using data augmentation and MuQ,\na pretrained feature-extraction model for music audio, to enhance\ngeneralisation abilities and extract meaningful audio features. Results show that the proposed A2S model achieves 4.98% symbol\nerror rate (SER) on the Quartets collection for classical music, which\nsignificantly outperforms the 15.3% SER from the existing stateof-the-art [1]. Additionally, our model achieves 20.92% SER on\nthe SheetSage-A2S dataset for popular music, serving as a strong\nbenchmark for future research. The dataset, model, and code are\nmade publicly available at: https://github.com/Multimodal-MusicResearch-Lab/SheetSage2Kern_model.\n\nCCS Concepts\n• Information systems → Music retrieval; • Applied computing → Sound and music computing; • Computing methodologies\n→ Feature selection.\n\nKeywords\nAudio-to-Score Transcription, Datasets, Lead Sheets, Pre-trained\nAudio Features\nACM Reference Format:\nEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju. 2026. Audio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features,\nData Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset. In Proceedings\nof the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM ’26), November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil. ACM, New York, NY, USA, 9 pages.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835653\n\n∗ Also with Great Bay University.\n† Corresponding author: juyaolong@gbu.edu.cn\n\nCopyright held by the owner/author(s). This is the author’s version of the work. It is posted here for\nyour personal use. Not for redistribution. The definitive Version of Record was published in\nProceedings of the 34th ACM International Conference on Multimedia (MM ’26),\nhttps://doi.org/10.1145/3767308.3835653.\n\nSymbolic\nRepresentation\n\nAudio File\n\nNote-level\nAMT\nModel\n\nScore\nDecoder\nA#dim7\n\nA2S\nModel\n\nF#7/A#\n\nSymbolic Score\n(kern, MusicXML, MEI,...)\n\nFigure 1: Illustration of the two main ways to generate symbolic music scores: (top) modular approach based on a traditional, note-level AMT model that typically outputs symbolic\nrepresentations such as MIDI or piano-roll; (bottom) A2S approach that directly generates score symbols based on the\ninput audio file.\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nAudio-to-Score Transcription (A2S) can be considered as a subfield\nof Automatic Music Transcription (AMT). Indeed, as defined in\n[3], AMT consists in “the design of computational algorithms to\nconvert acoustic music signals into some form of musical notation”.\nHowever, more often than not, the output of AMT models is a\nMIDI or piano-roll representation [3, 14], and not a full-fledged\nmusical score. A2S research addresses this limitation, by setting\nthe desired output as a human-readable score. In this section, we\nfirst introduce related work in the field of A2S and limitations of\nexisting approaches, before presenting our proposed approach and\ncontributions.\n\n1.1\n\nRelated Work\n\nObtaining a formatted score can be done in a modular or end-to-end\napproach, as illustrated in Figure 1. Modular approaches involve\nconverting a MIDI or piano-roll intermediate representation to\nsymbolic scores. This can be done with rule-based algorithms, as\nin [9] where the authors compute meter, key signature, and note\nspellings from MIDI files, and engrave the scores with Lilypond.\nMIDI-to-score conversion can also be formulated as a data-driven,\nsequence-to-sequence problem, as in [4], where a transformer\nmodel produces MusicXML files from performance MIDI recordings.\nA limitation of modular approaches is that they can accumulate\nerrors throughout their processing stages, unlike end-to-end approaches where audio is directly converted into a typeset score.\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n\nTable 1: Datasets used in A2S research. RISM files are incipits of unknown durations. All datasets are Western classical music\nscores except MuseSyn that includes piano arrangements of other musical styles, and our new dataset that gathers lead sheets\nof popular music. Although our dataset contains audio clips with singing vocals and accompaniment, a polyphonic texture\nsimilar to the Quartets, Chorales, and MuseSyn datasets, the corresponding scores of SheetSage-A2S contain the monophonic\nsinging melodies as the essential part of lead sheets.\nDataset\n\nNum. Files\n\nDuration\n\nRecordings\n\nScore Formats\n\nMusic Texture\n\nRISM [26]\n\n246,870\n\nUnknown\n\nSynthetic\n\nPAE [7] incipits\n\nMonophonic\n\nQuartets [25]\n\n34,512\n\n20.25h\n\nSynthetic\n\n**kern\n\nPolyphonic\n\nChorales [25]\n\n352\n\n5.79h\n\nSynthetic\n\n**kern\n\nPolyphonic\n\nMuseSyn [18]\n\n210\n\n9.62h\n\nSynthetic\n\nMusicXML\n\nPolyphonic\n\nSheetSage-A2S\n(Ours)\n\n9,468\n\n61h\n\nReal\n\n**kern\n\nPolyphonic\n\nConvolutional and recurrent neural networks have been used in\n[8, 24–26] for such end-to-end conversions, as well as attentionbased models in [1, 17].\nThe current A2S research faces two major limitations. One is\nthe lack of datasets, which has been highlighted in recent papers\n[2, 26], as many datasets include either audio files or symbolic\nscores but rarely both.1 One way of circumventing this problem is\nto generate audio files from symbolic scores using audio synthesis\nwith virtual instrument sounds. The humdrum-data repository2 is\ntherefore sometimes used as a source of music scores in the **kern\nformat [28] to build synthetic A2S datasets such as Quartets and\nChorales, used in [1, 2, 25]. Other public music scores websites\nlike the RISM and Musescore catalogues are used in [18, 26], but\nthe audio files are still obtained through digital synthesis. While\nsynthesis facilitates dataset creation, resorting to such recordings is\na significant drawback that questions the ability of previous work to\ngeneralise to real recordings. Furthermore, all the aforementioned\npapers study Western Classical Music, leaving the vast genre of\nPopular Music mostly untouched.\nTable 1 summarises the characteristics of datasets used in previous A2S research.\nThe other limitation is the lack of exploiting popular machine\nlearning techniques to improve performance, such as the use of\npre-trained models and data augmentation.\nPre-trained models proved useful in AMT, as [23] finetune a\nmodel trained on piano music and attain state-of-the-art performance on guitar music. The student-teacher learning paradigm can\nalso be used and showed promising results in drums transcription\n[34]. Recently, new music foundation models have also been proposed, which offer learned representations that can be applied to\ndownstream tasks [19]. Choosing the right pre-trained model for\na downstream task is not straightforward, which is why benchmarks like MARBLE [36] are useful to assess the performance of\na model. This benchmark uses 12 datasets to evaluate models on\n18 musical understanding tasks like key detection, chord estimation or source separation. Examples of models performing well on\n\nInstruments\nPiano, Harpsichord,\nOrgan, Strings, Winds\nString quartets\nSoprano, Alto, Tenor,\nBass voices\nPiano\nSinging vocals for\nmelodies\n\nMARBLE are MERT [16], MusicFM [33], and MuQ [38]. All three\nmodels are trained using masked language modelling, but with\ndifferent tokenisation strategies. Using a music foundation model is\na promising way of improving A2S models, as transcribing recordings to human-readable scores involves several complex processes,\nsuch as pitch and chord recognition, beat detection, and key finding.\nRegarding data augmentation, common techniques consist of applying controlled pitch-shifting or time-stretching to audio recordings\n[21]. These augmentations were shown to increase performance in\nAMT-related tasks, including transcriptions of lyrics [37], drums\n[13], piano [11], or classical music pieces with varied instrumentations [29]. Nevertheless, we could not find A2S papers that use\neither pre-trained models or such data augmentation techniques.\n\n1.2\n\nContributions\n\nIn this paper, we address the data scarcity limitation by proposing\nthe new SheetSage-A2S dataset, which includes 61 hours of audio\nwith 9,468 **kern score encodings for 6,066 unique songs,3 the\nfirst of its kind to facilitate A2S research for popular music. The\nscores in SheetSage-A2S are lead sheets, i.e. scores that engrave the\nmelody and chords for popular music and jazz songs [10, 20]. A lead\nsheet excerpt is shown on the right of Figure 1, where the melody\nis specified in the staff while chords are attached above the melody.\nUnlike existing A2S datasets, all music from our SheetSage-A2S\ndataset contains real recordings released commercially that can\nbe found on YouTube, or other music streaming services. Another\ndifference is that our dataset isolates the melody tracks, which\nare all sung by singers, and exclusively records their information\nfrom polyphonic mixtures, in contrast to the existing databases\nthat transcribe and notate every individual note of relevant musical\ninstruments.\nWe also address the performance ceiling by proposing our new\nA2S model, which adopts an autoregressive transformer decoder\nsimilar to [1], and make three modifications (shown in\nFigure 5\n). First, we increase the capacity and stability of the original decoder by expanding its feedforward dimension from 256 to 1024 and\n\n1 See for instance https://www.audiocontentanalysis.org/datasets (all web links were\n\nlast accessed and verified in March 2026).\n2 https://github.com/humdrum-tools/humdrum-data\n\n3 Each encoding represents a section (e.g. chorus, verse) of the song. Therefore, multiple\n\nencodings may belong to the same song.\n\n\fAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n\nData Acquisition\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nOctave Inference\n\nKern Conversion\n\nPitch map creation\nClip\n\nKern\nannotations\n\nHypothesis\nselection\n\nRMVPE\nprobabilities\n\nVocal stem\nextraction\n\nOctave\nfrom best\nhypothesis\nchosen\n\nRMS >\n0.0005?\nDownload\naudio\n\nC > 0.2?\nJson2Kern\nHypothesis generation\n\nExisting JSON from [10]\nwith refined beat timings\n\n12 hypotheses\n\nFigure 2: Overview of the SheetSage-A2S dataset creation pipeline, with three main steps that are introduced in detail at\nsection 2. The confidence score 𝐶 measures how well a potential octave and starting beat align with RMVPE’s [31] vocal pitch\nestimates. The process of Json2Kern is further illustrated in Figure 3.\nadopting a Pre-Norm architecture. Second, we replace the vanilla\nCNN encoder with MuQ [38], a pre-trained model that employs\nself-supervised learning to extract rich audio features for music understanding. Finally, we employ data augmentation, including pitch\nshifting and time stretching, to improve robustness and increase\nthe amount of data for training.\nThe experimental results demonstrate that the proposed A2S\nmodel achieves significant performance improvements over the\ncurrent state-of-the-art method [1] on the Quartets dataset for\nclassical music. Meanwhile, it obtains a Symbol Error Rate (SER)\nof 20.92% on the new SheetSage-A2S dataset for popular music,\nestablishing a strong benchmark for future research in this domain.\nThe organisation of the rest of this paper is described hereafter. Section 2 presents the SheetSage-A2S dataset. Section 3 describes the baseline model and our proposed architecture. Section 4\npresents the experiments, and Section 5 reports the results and ablation studies. Section 6 discusses the results, and Section 7 concludes\nthe paper and proposes directions for future research.\n\n2\n\nDataset\n\nWe introduce SheetSage-A2S, a dataset for popular music with\naudio-to-score transcription built from the original SheetSage annotations [10] and corresponding audio. At SheetSage, each annotation represents a distinct segment (such as a verse or chorus) from\na particular song. We therefore refer to these individual segments\nas “clips”. The dataset contains 9,468 clips derived from 6,066 songs\nby 2,891 artists. The mean clip duration is approximately 23 seconds, yielding 61 hours of audio in total. Each example pairs an\naudio clip with a **kern score obtained from the source annotations, and we choose **kern as the score encoding format due to\nits simple and compact form, compared to other formats such as\nMusicXML.4 Our dataset release contains no copyrighted audio: we\n4 https://www.musicxml.com/\n\ndistribute the **kern annotations under CC-BY-NC-ND 4.0 (similar\nto the original SheetSage), along with YouTube links, timestamps,\nand durations. We also provide pre-computed audio features for\nall clips. The whole dataset creation pipeline for SheetSage-A2S\nis shown in Figure 2, where we first acquire the data from SheetSage (Section 2.1), then we clean the data by removing all the clips\nwithout melodies or chords. For the remaining clips, we obtain the\npitches for all singing melodies via the process of octave inference\n(Section 2.2). Finally, we compile all relevant music elements for\neach clip from the JSON file and convert them into the resulting\n**kern annotations (Section 2.3), which are combined with the\ndownloaded audio to form the proposed SheetSage-A2S dataset.\n\n2.1\n\nData Acquisition\n\nWe follow [10] and use their open-source JSON file,5 which contains\nclips of annotations as well as the corresponding YouTube links for\nmusic audio, as a starting point. We attempt to scrape all the audio\nfiles with YouTube links using the yt-dlp6 toolkit. There are 26,175\nentries in the original JSON file, and we managed to download\n20,072 audio clips. Among the 6,103 failed cases, around 4000 clips\nare not available anymore, the remaining ones are not made public\ndue to copyright reasons.\nWithin the JSON file provided by [10], the onsets and offsets\nfor each note and chord are specified in terms of musical beats\nrather than exact timestamps in seconds. To align these symbolic\nannotations with the downloaded audio, the JSON includes refined\nbeat-to-time mappings generated using the beat and downbeat\ndetection algorithms [6] from the madmom library [5], which has\nbeen demonstrated to be highly reliable for mapping beat positions\nto precise timestamps in seconds. We adopt the formalisation from\n5 https://github.com/chrisdonahue/sheetsage-data/blob/main/hooktheory/\n\nHooktheory.json.gz\n6 https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n\n[10] and define this function as an alignment function 𝐴 : [0, 𝐵) →\n[0,𝑇 ) that assigns each of 𝐵 beats in the metrical structure to a time\n𝑡 ∈ [0,𝑇 ) in the audio.\n\n2.2\n\njson\n\nOctave Inference\n\n𝑡𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 (𝑠) = 𝐴(𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 ) − 𝑠,\n\n𝑡𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 (𝑠) = 𝐴(𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 ) − 𝑠\n\nEach combination of (𝑂, 𝑠) defines a hypothesis index mask: the\nset of cells in 𝑃 that the notes would occupy under that hypothesis,\nshown as the red rectangles in Figure 2:\n𝐻 (𝑂, 𝑠) =\n\nØ\b\n\nMetadata\n\nMelody\n\nOne obstacle encountered during the step of data acquisition is that\nthe melodies written on the original SheetSage annotation use a\nrelative octave system rather than an absolute one.7 As a result, the\nexact pitch of each note from the melody is unknown, preventing\nus from obtaining symbolic scores.\nPitch map creation. To overcome this issue and obtain absolute\noctave information, we use existing pitch estimation tools to obtain\nfundamental frequencies (F0) for singing melodies,8 which are first\nextracted using a\nsource separation tool known as the Mel-band Roformer [30]\nfor each audio clip. We then eliminate all silent clips and retain\nthe ones whose singing RMS (Root Mean Square) energy exceeds\n0.0005, resulting in 16,310 candidate clips. Next, we adopt RMVPE\n[31] as our pitch estimation tool and apply it to the isolated singing\nmelodies, resulting in\n72 semitone-spaced bins\nthat form a pitch probability matrix 𝑃 ∈ [0, 1]𝑇 ×72 , where T\ndenotes the number of time frames and the y-axis contains semitonespaced frequency probabilities from the note range within C1-B6.\nHypothesis generation. Let 𝑁 denote the set of annotated\nnotes in the annotated melody, where each note 𝑛 ∈ 𝑁 has a relative\npitch 𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑙,𝑛 and beat-indexed onset and offset [𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 , 𝑏𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 ]. Each\nrelative pitch 𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑙,𝑛 is made up of the note’s pitch class (an integer\nfrom 0 to 11) and the note’s relative octave (an integer typically\nbetween -1 and 1, which allows users to enter for example an\nE and then an E the next octave up or down). To determine the\nmost probable octave, we first treat the note’s relative pitch as\nan absolute pitch. We then shift it upwards by an octave for all\noctaves within RMVPE’s output range. We test all combinations\nof six octave shifts 𝑂 ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5} and two start time offsets\n𝑠: the madmom detected downbeat start timestamp and the userdefined start timestamp. Since the alignment function 𝐴 maps beats\nto global time, but our pitch matrix 𝑃 is for a trimmed clip, we\nsubtract 𝑠 to find the notes’ local positions. We test both offsets\nbecause madmom can sometimes miss the first beat (e.g. if a clip\nbegins with a held note), which shifts the entire melody.\nFor each note we then calculate the start and end time as:\n\n(𝑡, 𝑝𝑟𝑒𝑙,𝑛 + 12 · 𝑂) | 𝑡 ∈ [𝑡𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 (𝑠), 𝑡𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 (𝑠)]\n\n𝑛∈𝑁\n7 This means that the annotation only contains the pitch class and its octave relative to\n\nother notes.\n8We choose to focus singing melodies rather than melodies performed by instruments\nin this work, because existing pitch estimation tools [15, 31, 32] only used synthesised\ninstruments for testing, which may not generalise well on our dataset that contain\nreal recordings for commercial music.\n\nPitch classes, onsets\nand offsets\n\nNote spelling\n\ntitle\nartist\nhooktheory-id\nyoutube-url\nkey\n\n1\n\nChords\nRoot notes, intervals,\ninversions, onsets and\noffsets\n\nRoot note\nspelling\nOther tones\nspelling\n\nmeter\n\nChord labelling\n\nTemporal\nAlignment\nRhythm\nQuantisation\n\n2\n\n3\n\n[header]\n\n**kern\n[melody\nspine]\n\n**cdata\n[chord\nspine]\n\n[footer]\n\n4 Symbolic Score\n\nFigure 3: Summary of the json to **kern conversion pipeline.\nNumbers denote the four main processing steps described in\nSection 2.3.\nCombining six octave shifts with two start time offsets gives 12\nhypotheses per clip.\nHypothesis selection. We score each hypothesis by the probability mass from 𝑃 that falls inside its mask, normalised by total\nnote duration:\nÍ\n(𝑡, 𝑝 ) ∈ 𝐻 𝑃 (𝑡, 𝑝)\n\u0001\n𝐶 (𝐻 ) = Í\n𝑡\n𝑛∈𝑁 𝑒𝑛𝑑,𝑛 (𝑠) − 𝑡𝑠𝑡𝑎𝑟𝑡,𝑛 (𝑠)\nThe starting time and octave from the hypothesis with the highest confidence are selected as the clip’s octave and starting time. We\ndiscard clips with confidence 𝐶 = argmax 𝐶 (𝐻 ) below a threshold\n𝐻\n\nof 0.2 for the following reasons. First, manual inspection revealed\nthat confidence scores below 0.2 indicated severe transcription errors or instances where the instrumental part, rather than singing\nmelodies, was transcribed.\nSecond, through a manual inspection of around 150 clips as a\nquality check, we did not find any that were assigned the incorrect\noctave with confidence above 0.2. As a result, we obtain the clips\nwith only singing vocals with correct absolute octave information,\nresulting in 9,468 clips that are ready to be converted as **kern\nscores in the following step.\n\n2.3\n\n**kern Conversion\n\nThe original SheetSage files are in JSON format. To use the files\nin our A2S pipeline, we convert them to the **kern9 format. This\nconversion process is illustrated in Figure 3 and based upon four\n9 https://www.humdrum.org/rep/kern/\n\n\f10 In music theory, enharmonic refers to two or more distinct note spellings (e.g. note\n\nnames with different accidentals) that correspond to the same exact pitch class in\ntwelve-tone equal temperament. For example, C♯ and D♭ are enharmonic equivalents: they sound identical, but are written differently in the score and serve different\nharmonic and contextual functions in a piece of music.\n11 https://www.hooktheory.com/theorytab, HookTheory is the Internet’s largest database of songs broken down by music theory, which is also the source of the original\nSheetSage annotations.\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\n(a) Clip Duration\n2000\n0\n\n0\n\n20\n\n40\n\n60\n\nSeconds\n\n80\n\n100\n\nNumber of clips\n\nmain steps: converting the melody notes, converting the chord\ninformation, aligning and quantising the rhythm of the melody and\nchords, and finally merging everything in a Humdrum **kern file.\n(1) Melody: The original JSON file represents the pitch class\nof each melodic note using an integer between 0 and 11, but this\nrepresentation is enharmonic10 and does not specify note spelling,\nwhich is essential for standard music score generation. From the\nperspective of music theory, note spellings should be consistent\nwith the underlying key signature [27].\nWe therefore extract the key signature information from the\nsource JSON files, and derive the note spellings from pitch classes\naccordingly.\n(2) Chords: Since the SheetSage-A2S dataset represents lead\nsheets, chords are annotated in addition to the melody. Chords\nare defined by root pitch class, interval content in semitones, and\ninversion. Root note spelling is obtained in the same way as melody\nnotes if it belongs to the scale. For out-of-scale notes, enharmonic\nspelling is chosen by matching the key’s alteration type (e.g. using\nsharps in a key with sharps like D Major). Other tones spelling is\nconducted with respect to the root note, matching the intervallic\ncontent of the chord to a dictionary of known chord natures to\nchoose the correct enharmonic spelling.\nThe final chord labelling step produces a label in Harte syntax\n[12] of the form <root>:<nature>[/bass], such as A:min7/3 for\nan A minor seventh chord in first inversion, for example.\nThe <bass> component of the chord label is omitted if it is identical to the root note, and the <nature> is obtained from matching\nthe intervallic content to a pre-existing chord database that was\nassembled as needed while preparing the dataset.\n(3) Temporal Alignment and Rhythmic Quantisation: The melody\nnotes and chord annotations are encoded independently, each carrying its own beat-indexed onset and offset values. For temporal\nalignment, we merge all note and chord events, then iterate over\nthe resulting time slices. At each slice, we build the **kern and\n**cdata spines by writing the melody token and the current chord\nsymbol as a tab-separated row, with a dot (.) filling any column\nwhere no new event begins. We then conduct rhythm quantisation\nby mapping the duration values to **kern rhythm tokens, using\nrational fractions with a maximum denominator of 96 to deal with\nfast grace notes. For example, durations of 1 and 0.5 (a quarter note\nand an eighth note) in the JSON file becomes respectively 4 and 8\nin **kern.\n(4) Symbolic Score: We finish the conversion by merging the\ntwo musical spines **kern (for the melody) and **cdata (for the\nchords) with a header and a footer. The header declares global\nattributes including clef, key and time signatures, as well as artist\nand clip metadata.\nThe footer appends bibliographical records including the original\nHookTheory11 and YouTube links.\n\nNumber of clips\n\nAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n\n(b) Inferred Octave\n\n5000\n0\n\n2\n\n3\n\n4\n\nOctave\n\n5\n\nFigure 4: SheetSage-A2S dataset statistics. (a) Clip duration,\ntruncated at 100 seconds; (b) Inferred absolute octave.\n\n2.4\n\nData Statistics\n\nThe 9,468 clips (Figure 4) have a mean duration of 23.36 seconds\nwith a standard deviation of ∼11.34 seconds; 12 clips exceed 100\nseconds.\nThe clips originate from 6,066 unique songs across 2,891 artists.\nWhile 3,635 songs are represented by a single clip, the remainder\ncontribute several. The songs per artist distribution is long-tailed:\n2,005 artists contribute only one song, with an overall mean of 2.1\nsongs per artist. A notable outlier is The Beatles, with 127 songs.\nUsing the octave inference process introduced in\nSection 2.2 ,\n98.66% of clips are assigned to octave three or four.\n\n3\n\nProposed Method\n\nThis section describes our proposed A2S method. We first detail\nthe core model architecture, including the baseline design and our\nenhancements leveraging a pre-trained audio foundation model.\nWe then discuss the\ndata augmentation techniques implemented.\n\n3.1\n\nModel Configuration\n\nBaseline Architecture. We start with the model from [1] shown\nin Figure 5(a). This model is a CNN encoder with a Transformer\ndecoder. The CNN encoder first turns an input spectrogram into a\n2D feature map. After adding 2D positional encodings, the feature\nmap is flattened to form the cross-attention memory passed to the\nTransformer decoder.\nProposed Architecture. Our newly-proposed A2S model is illustrated in Figure 5(b). To establish a stronger foundation, we first\nincrease the capacity of the Transformer decoder by expanding the\nTransformer decoder’s feedforward dimension from 256 to 1024\nand changing its normalisation order from post-norm to pre-norm\nin order to improve training stability [35]. We refer to this change as\nthe 1024-PreNorm baseline, which will be further studied in\nSection 5.1 . Then, we adopt the MuQ pretrained model [38] to replace\nthe vanilla CNN encoder as a feature extractor from audio files,\nto study whether it can mitigate data scarcity issues discussed in\nSection 1 . We utilise MuQ due to its strong overall performance\non the music understanding benchmark MARBLE [36], suggesting\nthat it learns useful representations for downstream music tasks.\nWe specially keep MuQ frozen and use its final layer’s hiddenstate sequence as output. We then use a linear projection followed\nby LayerNorm to reduce the MuQ embedding size from its original\n1024 to 256.\nAs MuQ outputs a one-dimensional sequence, we use a onedimensional sinusoidal positional encoding in place of the baseline’s\n2D one. Finally, the resulting features (note that MuQ outputs 25\nfeature vectors per second, compared to ∼5.38 per second from\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n\na 7× expansion of the training set. We use Rubber Band12 for both\npitch shifting and time stretching.\n\n(a) Previous baseline\n2D Positional\nEncoding\n\nAudio Signal\nCNN\nEncoder\n\nFlatten\n\n4\n\nSpectrogram\nNorm\nAdd\n256D FFN\n\n1D\nPositional\nEncoding\n\nNorm\nAdd\nCross Attention\n\n256D\nEmbedding\n\nNorm\n\nAdd\nSelf Attention\n\nPreviously\npredicted tokens\n\nOutput\nProbabilities\nNotation\nProjection\n\n4.1\n(b) Our proposed model\n1D Positional\nEncoding\nMuQ\n(Frozen)\n\nLinear\nProjection +\nLayerNorm\n\nAdd\n1024D FFN\nNorm\n\n1D\nPositional\nEncoding\n\nAdd\n\n256D\nEmbedding\n\nAdd\nCross Attention\nNorm\n\nSelf Attention\nNorm\n\nPreviously\npredicted tokens\n\nOutput\nProbabilities\nNotation\nProjection\n\n8x Transformer Decoder Layer\n\nArgmax\n\nFigure 5: Comparison of our proposed architecture against\nthe baseline of [1]. Both models use an autoregressive Transformer decoder to generate **kern score symbols. We replace\nthe CNN encoder from the baseline with MuQ [38], a pretrained model to extract audio features, followed by a linear projection and LayerNorm to map MuQ’s embedding\ndimension from 1024 to 256. We also increase the decoder\nfeedforward dimension from 256 to 1024, and change its normalisation order from post-norm to pre-norm.\n\nthe baseline CNN encoder) are then input as the cross-attention\nmemory to the Transformer decoder.\n\n3.2\n\nWe conduct experiments on two datasets: the Quartets collection\nand our new SheetSage-A2S dataset. We provide implementation\ndetails for training and testing the baseline model and our proposed\narchitecture.\n\n8x Transformer Decoder Layer\n\nArgmax\n\nAudio Signal\n\nExperiments\n\nData Augmentation\n\nTo improve robustness and increase the amount of training data,\nwe apply offline data augmentation to the training split. We implement pitch-shifting and time-stretching, leaving the exploration of\nother augmentation techniques to future work. We denote pitch\nshifting by P𝑘 , where 𝑘 is measured in semitones, and time stretching by T𝑠 , where 𝑠 is the stretch factor. For each training pair\n(𝑥, 𝑦), we generate six augmented variants for each pitch shift\n𝑘 ∈ {−3, −2, −1, 1, 2, 3}. For each such variant, we independently\nsample a time-stretch factor 𝑠 ∈ {0.9, 0.95, 1.05, 1.1} and construct\naugmented audio 𝑥 ′ = T𝑠 (P𝑘 (𝑥)). These ranges were chosen to\nprovide the model with enough variety to learn to handle different\nfrequency ranges and tempos while ensuring the augmented audio\nstill sounds like a real performance.\nThe symbolic target is transposed accordingly, giving 𝑦 ′ = P𝑘 (𝑦).\nWe do not modify 𝑦 for T𝑠 on the SheetSage-A2S dataset. As the\ntarget representation uses relative note values and lacks an explicit\nmeter, it is tempo-invariant. However, for the Quartets dataset,\nthe tempo metadata is updated accordingly. The original training\nexample is retained alongside its augmented variants, resulting in\n\nConfigurations\n\nQuartets Dataset. We evaluate our approach on the Quartets collection, which comprises string quartets by Mozart, Beethoven, and\nHaydn, serving as a direct point of comparison with [1]. The dataset\nis partitioned into 70% training, 15% validation, and 15% test sets,\nconsistent with [1]. For our experiments on this dataset, we train\na baseline A2S model using the original word-level tokenisation.\nWe then incrementally incorporate MuQ, the pre-trained encoder\nand our data augmentation strategy to evaluate their individual\ncontributions.\nSheetSage-A2S Dataset. Following the methodology in [10], this\ndataset employs an artist-stratified split of 80% training, 10% validation, and 10% testing.\nFor both datasets, the audio files were resampled to 22,050 Hz\nwhen using the baseline architecture, following [1], and to 24,000\nHz when using MuQ [38]. In both cases, we first train a baseline\nA2S model. We then evaluate the incremental improvements from\nincorporating MuQ and our data augmentation strategy.\n\n4.2\n\nImplementation Details\n\nEvaluation. Symbol Error Rate (SER) computes the Levenshtein\ndistance between ground truth and prediction: the minimum number of single-element edits (insertions, deletions, or substitutions)\nrequired to transform the predicted sequence into the target sequence. We use SER as our primary metric for both datasets, with\nall sequences generated using greedy decoding at inference time.\nThis ensures direct compatibility with existing A2S work [1], and\nalso provides a consistent baseline against which future models\ntrained on SheetSage-A2S can be evaluated. While MV2H [22] has\nbeen used in other audio-to-score work, current implementations\ndo not support the lead sheet format we generated in Section 2.3.\nWe therefore provide both a per-spine SER breakdown and SER\nscores for both melody and chord instead.\nTraining. Models are trained using the AdamW optimiser with a\nweight decay of 1e-3, a constant learning rate of 5e-5, and a batch\nsize of 8. This contrasts with the original training setup (Adam, no\nweight decay, lr 1e-4, batch size 1).\nWe made this switch as we found through empirical testing that\nit improved training stability. We use an early-stopping patience of\n5 epochs on the validation loss with a minimum delta of 1e-3, and\nretain the best model.\nOn both datasets the model is trained with cross-entropy loss.\nAs the SheetSage-A2S annotations are user-generated and contain\noccasional errors, we apply label smoothing of 0.1 while training on\nSheetSage-A2S, which softens the target distribution and reduces\n12 https://breakfastquay.com/rubberband/\n\n\fAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n\nTable 2: Symbol error rate (SER) for each proposed modification, with a granular breakdown (melody and chord SERs)\non SheetSage-A2S. Each row adds one component to the baseline of [1]. The spine-level metrics exclude structural tokens\n(tabs and newlines), which are comparatively easy to predict,\nand are therefore expected to be higher than the overall SER.\nMelody and chord SERs are not provided for the Quartets\ndataset, as it doesn’t contain chord annotations, and melodic\ncontent can span multiple distinct spines.\n\nVariant\n\nSER\n\nBaseline [1]\n+1024-Pre-Norm\n+MuQ encoder\n+Augmented data\n\n66.85\n53.7\n25.39\n20.92\n\nSheetSage-A2S\nMelody SER Chord SER\n106.88\n90.56\n46.21\n38.62\n\n64.12\n51.8\n26.76\n22.28\n\nQuartets\nSER\n15.3\n8.48\n7.16\n4.98\n\nthe penalty for deviating from potentially incorrect ground truth\nlabels. We found this label smoothing provided a small SER decrease.\n\nSER on SheetSage-A2S. While songs in SheetSage-A2S may overlap with the MSD, quantifying this is non-trivial due to naming\ninconsistencies across the datasets. However, the consistent gains\nfrom MuQ on the stylistically distinct Quartets corpus suggest the\nimprovements stem from generally useful learned representations.\nTokenisation. To determine the most effective tokenisation strategy, we conducted an ablation study comparing the three methods\nproposed by [20]: word-level, mid-level, and character-level tokenisation. All experiments were run using our MuQ architecture prior\nto applying data augmentation. As shown in Table 3, word-level\ntokenisation outperformed both alternatives. Although mid-level tokenisation performed best in [20] and offers theoretical advantages\nsuch as a bijective mapping between graphical symbols and tokens,\nand a reduced vocabulary size, these advantages did not translate\nto our dataset. We partly attribute this to the long sequence lengths\npresent in the SheetSage-A2S dataset, where the reduced sequence\nlength produced by word-level tokenisation seems more beneficial.\nTable 3: Performances of different tokenisation methods\non the SheetSage-A2S dataset. The model used is the MuQ\nvariant without augmentation.\n\n5 Results\n5.1 Transcription Performance\nThe transcription performance on both datasets is detailed in Table 2, using the word-level tokenisation scheme identical to the\nbaseline model [1]. On Quartets, our proposed model achieves an\nSER of 4.98, representing a 67.5% relative reduction from the baseline SER of 15.3.\nFor a more granular and musically meaningful evaluation, we\nalso include the SER per spine, namely melody and chord SER\nresults for our SheetSage-A2S dataset in Table 2. Every proposed\nmodification improves all of these scores. Note that per-spine SER\nis computed excluding structural tokens (such as tab and newline),\nwhich are relatively easier to predict, so per-spine SER values are\nexpected to be higher than normal SER. SER can also exceed 100%.\n\n5.2\n\nAblation study\n\nEncoder and augmentation. To evaluate the individual contributions\nof the proposed methods, in Table 2 we analyse the performance of\nintermediate models bridging the baseline [1] and the final architecture. The evaluation isolates three primary modifications: the\nmodel architecture finetuning, the integration of the MuQ feature\nextractor, and the application of data augmentation.\nFirst, our 1024-PreNorm baseline (see\nSection 3.1 ) achieves\nan SER of 8.48 on the Quartets dataset and 53.7 on SheetSage-A2S.\nBased on our preliminary experiments, we primarily attribute the\nimprovement of the original model’s 15.3 SER [1] on Quartets and\n66.85 on SheetSage-A2S to the increased feedforward dimension\nfrom 256 to 1024. Next, replacing the vanilla CNN encoder with\nfrozen MuQ features further reduces the Quartets SER to 7.16. More\nnotably, for SheetSage-A2S, adding MuQ decreases the SER to 25.39.\nThe larger performance gain on SheetSage-A2S is expected, as the\nMuQ model we utilise was pre-trained on the Million Song Dataset\n(MSD), a large corpus of popular music. Finally, applying data augmentation yields our best results: a 4.98 SER on Quartets and 20.92\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nMethod\nCharacter level\nMid level\nWord level\n\n6\n\nSymbol Error Rate\n33.97\n30.44\n25.39\n\nToken count\n61\n224\n4763\n\nDiscussion\n\nIn this section, we discuss some limitations observed from using the\nSheetSage-A2S dataset, in particular due to the non-standardised\nnature of the annotations. We then propose a qualitative analysis\nof a transcription result from our best performing model to reflect\nupon the strengths and weaknesses of our approach.\n\n6.1\n\nUser-Annotation Variabilities\n\nA contributing factor to the higher SER on the SheetSage-A2S\ndataset is the inherent variability of user-generated content. Transcribers slightly differ in subtle stylistic choices, which the model\nhas no way of discerning from the audio. We choose to release\nthe core dataset with minimal preprocessing. However, this lack of\nstandardisation is likely to introduce performance penalties.\nFirst, users transcribe melodies and chords with varying standards. Some annotators label a chord only once when the harmony\nchanges, while others annotate the same chord repeatedly for every\nrhythmic strum, or once per bar. Furthermore, we observe issues\nwith inconsistent transcriptions, where some users stop transcribing before the audio clip officially ends as the remaining (usually\nsmall) part does not belong to the current music section, which\npenalises the model for correctly transcribing the remaining audio.\nSome users also transcribe small parts of the accompaniment when\nvocals are silent, a behaviour our model did not catch on as it is not\nprevalent in the training data.\nSecond, melodies expect different levels of rhythmic granularity.\nThis is partly due to user annotation inconsistencies: some users\nannotate separate notes for every word, some for every syllable,\n\n\fMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nEoin Cummins, Zhongyi Huang, Alexandre D’Hooge, Zhuoro Mo, and Yaolong Ju\n\nissues are especially common when the same pitch is repeated\nmultiple times in succession (common for example, in rap verse\nannotations). Furthermore, the model can misidentify note offsets,\neither cutting a note short to insert an incorrect rest, or holding\na note slightly too long through a space where a brief rest should\noccur. This error is shown in Figure 6 in green, where in the second measure the model terminates the half note prematurely by\ninserting an incorrect rest.\nUltimately, while this internal reshuffling causes the exact start\nand end times of individual notes to shift, the overall phrase length\nremains intact, and these timing deviations rarely exceed an eighth\nnote. Musically, these offset errors are minor issues as even human\ntranscribers might disagree on smooth offsets like those of singing\nvocals. Other than that, the model excels at predicting the essential\nmusical elements, including pitches, note onsets, chord root, and\nthe overall musical structure, which align well with the original\naudio.\n\n7\nFigure 6: Measures 5 to 7 from Love Live - Bokutachi wa Hitotsu no Hikari.12 It demonstrates that our best model can\nhave trouble determining exact note-rest boundaries and\nnon-triad chord labels.\n\nand others only annotate when the pitch changes. Furthermore,\nsingers often employ expressive vocal techniques, such as subtle\npitch bends or grace notes. Some users annotate both the start\nand end of the bend, while others only annotate the core note. All\nthe aforementioned cases serve as inconsistencies in the training\nand evaluation processes that compromise the performances of our\nmodel on the SheetSage-A2S dataset.\n\n6.2\n\nQualitative analysis\n\nTo further illustrate the model’s behaviour on the SheetSage-A2S\ndataset, we present a qualitative analysis of the transcription results\ngenerated by our best-performing model (MuQ variant with data\naugmentation), along with the ground truth shown in Figure 6.\nFor chord transcription, the model typically identifies root notes\ncorrectly but has difficulty labelling chords beyond simple triads.\nAdditionally, the model can sporadically label the inversion of an\ninverted chord as the root of the chord. Finally, in cases where a\nsong maintains the same root but cycles through subtly different\nchord qualities, the model tends to sustain the quality of its initial\nchord prediction without registering the subsequent changes. In\nFigure 6, for instance, the last Eb:sus4 is not identified and the\nEb:11 is maintained. The disambiguation between these chords\ncan however be subtle as both contain a perfect fourth (called\n11th when an octave higher) as a characteristic tone. Overall, the\nmodel successfully captures the overarching chord progression but\nsimplifies the first F:min7 chord to a F:min.\nThe model’s most significant difficulty lies in predicting the exact\nnote durations, indicated as Timing Issue in Figure 6.\nTwo examples are note consolidation (merging consecutive notes)\nand note separation (splitting a single note). We observed that these\n12 https://hookpad.hooktheory.com/?idOfSong=yvmrlMLVxOW - measures 5-7\n\nConclusion and Future Work\n\nIn this paper, we first introduce the new SheetSage-A2S Dataset, a\ndedicated lead sheet corpus for supporting A2S research in the style\nof popular music. It contains 61 hours of audio for 6,066 unique\nsongs, paired with **kern lead sheet annotations. We improve\nexisting A2S frameworks by using data augmentation and MuQ [38],\na pre-trained music feature extractor, to enhance generalisation\nand capture high-quality semantic audio features. Experiments\nshow our A2S system significantly outperforms the existing stateof-the-art [1] for classical music, and achieves 20.92% SER on the\nSheetSage-A2S dataset for popular music, setting a competitive\nbaseline for future research.\nLooking ahead, we will expand the SheetSage-A2S dataset with\ninstrument-performed melody clips, as current octave inference\nonly retains clips with singing vocals.\nWhile this work preserves the users’ annotations with minimal\npre-processing to present the SheetSage-A2S dataset in its original\nform, we plan to resolve the annotation variabilities and inconsistencies for the better training and evaluation of A2S models. We\nwill also implement more advanced evaluation metrics, to better\nqualify the mistakes made by our model and their musical significance. Lastly, we plan to carry out an inter-annotator study to\nquantify the difference in annotation styles between annotations\nfor our clips.\n\nAcknowledgments\nThe computational resources are supported by SongShan Lake HPC\nCenter (SSL-HPC) in Great Bay University.\n\n\fAudio-to-Score Transcription using Pre-trained Features, Data Augmentation, and the New SheetSage-A2S Dataset\n\nReferences\n[1] María Alfaro-Contreras, Antonio Ríos-Vila, Jose J. Valero-Mas, and Jorge CalvoZaragoza. 2024. A Transformer Approach for Polyphonic Audio-to-Score Transcription. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech\nand Signal Processing (ICASSP). 706–710.\n[2] Victor Arroyo, Jose J. Valero-Mas, Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza, and Antonio Pertusa.\n2022. Neural Audio-To-Score Music Transcription For Unconstrained Polyphony\nUsing Compact Output Representations. In Proceedings of the IEEE International\nConference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). 4603–4607.\n[3] Emmanouil Benetos, Simon Dixon, Zhiyao Duan, and Sebastian Ewert. 2019.\nAutomatic Music Transcription: An Overview. IEEE Signal Processing Magazine\n36, 1 (2019), 20–30.\n[4] Tim Beyer and Angela Dai. 2024. End-to-End Piano Performance-MIDI to Score\nConversion with Transformers. In Proceedings of the 25th International Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 319–326.\n[5] Sebastian Böck, Filip Korzeniowski, Jan Schlüter, Florian Krebs, and Gerhard\nWidmer. 2016. madmom: A New Python Audio and Music Signal Processing\nLibrary. In Proceedings of the 24th ACM International Conference on Multimedia.\n1174–1178.\n[6] Sebastian Böck, Florian Krebs, and Gerhard Widmer. 2016. Joint Beat and Downbeat Tracking with Recurrent Neural Networks. In Proceedings of the 17th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 255–261.\n[7] Barry S Brook. 1965. The simplified\" plaine and easie code system\" for notating\nmusic: a proposal for international adoption. Fontes artis musicae 12, 2/3 (1965),\n156–160.\n[8] Ralf Gunter Correa Carvalho and Paris Smaragdis. 2017. Towards End-to-end\nPolyphonic Music Transcription: Transforming Music Audio Directly to a Score.\nIn IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to Audio and Acoustics\n(WASPAA). 151–155.\n[9] Andrea Cogliati, David Temperley, and Zhiyao Duan. 2016. Transcribing Human\nPiano Performances into Music Notation. In Proceedings of the 17th International\nSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 758–764.\n[10] Chris Donahue, John Thickstun, and Percy Liang. 2022. Melody Transcription\nVia Generative Pre-Training. In Proceedings of the 23rd International Society for\nMusic Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 485–492.\n[11] Drew Edwards, Simon Dixon, Emmanouil Benetos, Akira Maezawa, and Yuta\nKusaka. 2024. A Data-Driven Analysis of Robust Automatic Piano Transcription.\nIEEE Signal Processing Letters 31 (2024), 681–685.\n[12] Christopher Harte, Mark Sandler, Samer Abdallah, and Emilia Gomez. 2005. Symbolic Representation of Musical Chords: A Proposed Syntax for Text Annotations.\nIn Proceedings of the 6th International Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConference (ISMIR). 66–71.\n[13] Celine Jacques and Axel Roebel. 2019. Data Augmentation for Drum Transcription\nwith Convolutional Neural Networks. In Proceedings of the 27th European Signal\nProcessing Conference (EUSIPCO). 1–5.\n[14] Fatemeh Jamshidi, Gary Pike, Amit Das, and Richard Chapman. 2024. Machine\nLearning Techniques in Automatic Music Transcription: A Systematic Survey. https:\n//arxiv.org/abs/2406.15249 A Preprint.\n[15] Jong Wook Kim, Justin Salamon, Peter Li, and Juan Pablo Bello. 2018. CREPE:\nA Convolutional Representation for Pitch Estimation. In Proceedings of the IEEE\nInternational Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP).\n161–165.\n[16] Yizhi Li, Ruibin Yuan, Ge Zhang, Yinghao Ma, Xingran Chen, Hanzhi Yin, Chenghao Xiao, Chenghua Lin, Anton Ragni, Emmanouil Benetos, Norbert Gyenge,\nRoger Dannenberg, Ruibo Liu, Wenhu Chen, Gus Xia, Yemin Shi, Wenhao Huang,\nZili Wang, Yike Guo, and Jie Fu. 2024. MERT: Acoustic Music Understanding\nModel with Large-Scale Self-supervised Training. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR). 1–24.\n[17] Lele Liu and Emmanouil Benetos. 2021. From Audio to Music Notation. In\nHandbook of Artificial Intelligence for Music: Foundations, Advanced Approaches,\nand Developments for Creativity. Springer International Publishing, 693–714.\n[18] Lele Liu, Veronica Morfi, and Emmanouil Benetos. 2021. Joint Multi-Pitch Detection and Score Transcription for Polyphonic Piano Music. In Proceedings of the\nIEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP).\n281–285.\n[19] Yinghao Ma, Anders Øland, Anton Ragni, Bleiz MacSen Del Sette, Charalampos\nSaitis, Chris Donahue, Chenghua Lin, Christos Plachouras, Emmanouil Benetos, Elona Shatri, Fabio Morreale, Ge Zhang, György Fazekas, Gus Xia, Huan\nZhang, Ilaria Manco, Jiawen Huang, Julien Guinot, Liwei Lin, Luca Marinelli, Max\nW. Y. Lam, Megha Sharma, Qiuqiang Kong, Roger B. Dannenberg, Ruibin Yuan,\nShangda Wu, Shih-Lun Wu, Shuqi Dai, Shun Lei, Shiyin Kang, Simon Dixon,\n\nMM ’26, November 10–14, 2026, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil\n\nWenhu Chen, Wenhao Huang, Xingjian Du, Xingwei Qu, Xu Tan, Yizhi Li, Zeyue\nTian, Zhiyong Wu, Zhizheng Wu, Ziyang Ma, and Ziyu Wang. 2024. Foundation\nModels for Music: A Survey. http://arxiv.org/abs/2408.14340 A Preprint.\n[20] Juan C. Martinez-Sevilla, Francesco Foscarin, Patricia Garcia-Iasci, David Rizo,\nJorge Calvo-Zaragoza, and Gerhard Widmer. 2025. Optical Music Recognition\nof Jazz Lead Sheets. In Proceedings of the 26th International Society for Music\nInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 696–702.\n[21] Brian McFee, Eric J Humphrey, and Juan P Bello. 2015. A Software Framework\nfor Musical Data Augmentation. In Proceedings of the 16th International Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 248–254.\n[22] Andrew McLeod and Mark Steedman. 2018. Evaluating Automatic Polyphonic\nMusic Transcription. In Proceedings of the 19th International Society for Music\nInformation Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 42–49.\n[23] Xavier Riley, Drew Edwards, and Simon Dixon. 2024. High Resolution Guitar\nTranscription Via Domain Adaptation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International\nConference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). 1051–1055.\n[24] Miguel A Román, Antonio Pertusa, and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza. 2018. An End-toEnd Framework for Audio-to-Score Music Transcription on Monophonic Excerpts.\nIn Proceedings of the 19th International Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConference (ISMIR). 34–41.\n[25] Miguel A Román, Antonio Pertusa, and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza. 2019. A Holistic\nApproach to Polyphonic Music Transcription with Neural Networks. In Proceedings of the 20th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference\n(ISMIR). 731–737.\n[26] Miguel A. Román, Antonio Pertusa, and Jorge Calvo-Zaragoza. 2020. Data\nRepresentations for Audio-to-Score Monophonic Music Transcription. Expert\nSystems with Applications 162 (2020), 113769.\n[27] Julian Rushton. 2001. Enharmonic. Grove Music Online, Vol. 1. Oxford University\nPress.\n[28] Craig Stuart Sapp. 2005. Online Database of Scores in the Humdrum File Format.\nIn Proceedings of the 6th International Society for Music Information Retrieval\nConference (ISMIR). 664–665.\n[29] John Thickstun, Zaid Harchaoui, Dean P. Foster, and Sham M. Kakade. 2018.\nInvariances and data augmentation for supervised music transcription. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal\nProcessing (ICASSP). 2241–2245.\n[30] Ju-Chiang Wang, Wei-Tsung Lu, and Jitong Chen. 2024. Mel-RoFormer for\nVocal Separation and Vocal Melody Transcription. In Proceedings of the 25th\nInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 454–461.\n[31] Haojie Wei, Xueke Cao, Tangpeng Dan, and Yueguo Chen. 2023. RMVPE: A\nrobust model for vocal pitch estimation in polyphonic music. In Proceedings of\nthe 24th Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association\n(Interspeech). 5421–5425.\n[32] Weixing Wei, Peilin Li, Yi Yu, and Wei Li. 2022. HarmoF0: Logarithmic scale\ndilated convolution for pitch estimation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International\nConference on Multimedia and Expo (ICME). 1–6.\n[33] Minz Won, Yun-Ning Hung, and Duc Le. 2024. A Foundation Model for Music\nInformatics. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics,\nSpeech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). 1226–1230.\n[34] Chih-Wei Wu and Alexander Lerch. 2017. Automatic Drum Transcription Using\nthe Student-Teacher Learning Paradigm with Unlabeled Music Data. In Proceedings of the 18th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference\n(ISMIR). 613–620.\n[35] Ruibin Xiong, Yunchang Yang, Di He, Kai Zheng, Shuxin Zheng, Chen Xing,\nHuishuai Zhang, Yanyan Lan, Liwei Wang, and Tieyan Liu. 2020. On Layer\nNormalization in the Transformer Architecture. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Machine Learning. 10524–10533.\n[36] Ruibin Yuan, Yinghao Ma, Yizhi Li, Ge Zhang, Xingran Chen, Hanzhi Yin, zhuo le,\nYiqi Liu, Jiawen Huang, Zeyue Tian, Binyue Deng, Ningzhi Wang, Chenghua Lin,\nEmmanouil Benetos, Anton Ragni, Norbert Gyenge, Roger Dannenberg, Wenhu\nChen, Gus Xia, Wei Xue, Si Liu, Shi Wang, Ruibo Liu, Yike Guo, and Jie Fu. 2023.\nMARBLE: Music audio representation benchmark for universal evaluation. In\nProceedings of the Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems\n(NeurIPS). 39626–39647.\n[37] Chen Zhang, Jiaxing Yu, LuChin Chang, Xu Tan, Jiawei Chen, Tao Qin, and Kejun\nZhang. 2022. PDAugment: Data Augmentation by Pitch and Duration Adjustments for Automatic Lyrics Transcription. In Proceedings of the 23rd International\nSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR). 454–461.\n[38] Haina Zhu, Yizhi Zhou, Hangting Chen, Jianwei Yu, Ziyang Ma, Rongzhi Gu, Yi\nLuo, Wei Tan, and Xie Chen. 2025. MuQ: Self-Supervised Music Representation\nLearning With Mel Residual Vector Quantization. IEEE Transa